{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a3dbc53e42cadfce", "type": "tool_result", "content": "0\tAcoustiTrace: When Plausible Sound Violates Physics\n1\tShiyang Li1,2 , Yuewen Cao2 , Yihao Liu2 , Yuandong Pu3 ,\n2\tBaochang Zhang4 , Xiaofei Li5 , Changqing Zou1,6\n3\t1\n4\tZhejiang University\n5\tShanghai Artificial Intelligence Laboratory\n6\t3\n7\tShanghai Jiao Tong University\n8\t4\n9\tBeihang University\n10\t5\n11\tSchool of Engineering, Westlake University\n12\t6\n13\tZhejiang Lab\n14\t12521030@zju.edu.cn\n15\t2\n16\t\n17\tVisual Expectation\n18\tShort Reverberation\n19\t\n20\tReverberation Mismatch\n21\t\n22\tVisual Expectation\n23\tStrong Attenuation\n24\t\n25\tA growing class of generative models jointly synthesizes\n26\tvideo and audio (Liu et al. 2024, 2026a; Wang et al. 2025;\n27\tLow, Wang, and Katyal 2025; HaCohen et al. 2026). Benchmark studies indicate that these approaches have made substantial progress in cross-modal semantic coherence and temporal synchronization (Mao et al. 2024; Hua et al. 2026).\n28\tNevertheless, perceptual plausibility and audio–video alignment do not guarantee fidelity to the underlying acoustic\n29\tprocesses (Li et al. 2026; Oh et al. 2026; Cui et al. 2026; Xie\n30\tet al. 2025). Such violations may include unnatural attack\n31\tor decay, mismatched reverberation, or incorrect distance attenuation. They may occur even when the expected sound is\n32\tpresent and apparently synchronized. This is precisely when\n33\tplausible sound violates physics.\n34\tTo assess such failures, recent work has expanded audio–\n35\tvideo evaluation to explicitly consider physical realism (Xie\n36\t\n37\tAudio Observation\n38\tWeak Attenuation\n39\t\n40\tWrong Attenuation\n41\t\n42\tTime\n43\t\n44\tIntroduction\n45\t\n46\tAudio Observation\n47\tLong Reverberation\n48\t\n49\tAmplitude\n50\t\n51\tRecent audio–video generators can produce semantically\n52\tplausible and apparently synchronized sound, yet may still\n53\tviolate the acoustic processes implied by visible events and environments. Existing benchmarks provide limited support for\n54\tattributing such violations to particular acoustic processes and\n55\tquantifying their severity. We introduce AcoustiTrace, a diagnostic benchmark that formalizes acoustic physical realism\n56\tin audio–video generation. AcoustiTrace organizes text-toaudio-video (T2AV) and image-to-audio-video (I2AV) evaluation around the acoustic process, covering sound generation, propagation environment, and acoustic reception through\n57\teight dimensions grounded in measurable acoustic quantities.\n58\tBased on these evaluation dimensions, we construct a largescale dataset organized around acoustic mechanisms, comprising real-world audio–video recordings and acoustically\n59\tannotated RGB-D observations, and use it to develop targeted\n60\tprompt suites and validated evaluators. Experiments reveal\n61\tthat even leading generators still struggle with fundamental\n62\tacoustic processes despite producing plausible sound events.\n63\tFinally, we show that the diagnostics AcoustiTrace provides\n64\tfor specific acoustic relations can guide model refinement toward more physically faithful audio and open new directions\n65\tfor incorporating acoustic principles into training objectives,\n66\treward modeling, and candidate selection.\n67\t\n68\tAmplitude\n69\t\n70\tarXiv:2608.02035v1 [cs.MM] 3 Aug 2026\n71\t\n72\tAbstract\n73\t\n74\tPhysical Expectation\n75\tSmooth Decay\n76\t\n77\tTime\n78\t\n79\tAudio Observation\n80\tIrregular Decay\n81\t\n82\tUnnatural Decay\n83\tFigure 1: Representative cases where plausible sound violates acoustic physics. AcoustiTrace systematically diagnoses\n84\tthese and additional acoustic failures across eight evaluation\n85\tdimensions.\n86\t\n87\tet al. 2025; Li et al. 2026; Cui et al. 2026). Yet a diagnostic gap remains: existing approaches do not systematically\n88\tcombine validated and interpretable acoustic measurements\n89\tto diagnose generative models. Consequently, they provide\n90\tlimited support for localizing failures within the acoustic process, quantifying the magnitude of departures from expected\n91\tacoustic relations, and translating these diagnoses into model\n92\trefinement.\n93\t\n94\t\fFrom Acoustic Process to Evaluation Dimensions\n95\tSound\n96\tGeneration\n97\t\n98\tEnergy\n99\t\n100\tMotion-Loudness\n101\t\n102\tStiffness\n103\t\n104\tLog Attack Time\n105\t\n106\tDamping\n107\t\n108\tImpact Decay\n109\t\n110\tReflection\n111\tAcoustic\n112\tProcess\n113\t\n114\tPropagation\n115\tEnvironment\n116\t\n117\tAlignment & Validation\n118\tEvaluator Design\n119\t·Building acoustic priors into evaluators.\n120\t\n121\tVideo\n122\t\n123\tRT60 Consistency\n124\t\n125\tEnvelope\n126\tDecay\n127\t𝐴 𝑡 = 𝐴0 𝑒 −𝛽𝑡\n128\t\n129\tSabine’s\n130\tInverse-Square\n131\tFormula\n132\tLaw\n133\t𝑉\n134\t1\n135\t…\n136\t𝑇60 = 0.161\n137\t𝐼(𝑟)∞ 2\n138\t𝐴\n139\t𝑟\n140\t\n141\tAbsorption\n142\tCausality\n143\t\n144\tAcoustic\n145\tReception\n146\t\n147\tReal A/V Samples\n148\t\n149\tSpreading\n150\t\n151\tCausality Violation\n152\tRange Attenuation\n153\tApproach Gain\n154\t\n155\tDistance\n156\t\n157\tLateral Stability\n158\t\n159\tAudio\n160\t\n161\tPrompts\n162\t·Grounded in real-world\n163\tscenes.\n164\t·Tailored to evaluation\n165\tdimensions.\n166\t\n167\tEvaluators\n168\t\n169\tGenerate & Evaluate\n170\t\n171\tFigure 2: Framework of AcoustiTrace. The benchmark organizes acoustic physical realism into sound generation, propagation\n172\tenvironment, and acoustic reception across eight evaluation dimensions. Real-world A/V data support prompt construction,\n173\tevaluator design, and validation. By embedding acoustic priors into specialized evaluators, AcoustiTrace supports systematic\n174\tdiagnosis of acoustic physical realism in joint A/V generation.\n175\tTo address this gap, we introduce AcoustiTrace, a diagnostic benchmark that formalizes acoustic physical realism\n176\tin terms of the classical sequence of sound generation, propagation through the environment, and reception by the listener\n177\t(Kinsler et al. 2000). As illustrated in Figure 2, AcoustiTrace\n178\ttranslates this decomposition into eight evaluation dimensions for text-to-audio-video (T2AV) and image-to-audiovideo (I2AV) generation. Each dimension tests an explicit\n179\tacoustic relation between observable visual evidence and\n180\ta measurable audio quantity. The resulting diagnostics are\n181\texpressed in interpretable acoustic terms using established\n182\tdescriptors of temporal envelopes, reverberation decay, and\n183\tacoustic propagation (Peeters et al. 2011; Schroeder 1965;\n184\tKinsler et al. 2000).\n185\tGuided by these dimensions, we curate a dataset organized around real-world acoustic processes and use it to develop the evaluators, which we validate with real-world and\n186\tground-truth anchors together with controlled perturbations.\n187\tThe dataset then informs 605-prompt T2AV and 748-prompt\n188\tI2AV suites targeting acoustic relations within the evaluators’\n189\tvalidated measurement scope. Using AcoustiTrace, we evaluate nine joint audio–video generators. Experiments reveal\n190\tthat even leading generators still struggle to faithfully model\n191\tacoustic relations across the acoustic process despite producing plausible sound events. This persistent gap highlights an\n192\turgent need to improve current models through acoustically\n193\tcleaner training subsets, explicit acoustic supervision, and\n194\tspecialized annotations targeting acoustic quantities.\n195\tTo explore one such direction, we conduct an initial intervention study by converting a diagnosed range attenuation\n196\terror into a guidance signal for audio sampling. With the\n197\tmodel parameters, prompt, and video stream held fixed, the\n198\tguidance improves consistency with the targeted acoustic relation in 80.16% of valid samples. This proof of concept\n199\tdemonstrates that the validated and interpretable diagnostics provided by AcoustiTrace can identify concrete targets\n200\tand guide model refinement, opening broader opportunities\n201\t\n202\tfor physics-aware training objectives, reward modeling, and\n203\tcandidate selection.\n204\tOur contributions are summarized as follows:\n205\t1. We introduce AcoustiTrace, a diagnostic benchmark that\n206\torganizes the evaluation of joint audio–video generation\n207\taround the acoustic process. Across eight evaluation dimensions, AcoustiTrace uses validated and interpretable\n208\tacoustic measurements to identify and quantify deviations\n209\tfrom expected acoustic relations.\n210\t2. We curate a dataset centered on acoustic processes, comprising 11,296 unique real-world audio–video samples\n211\tand 82,828 RGB-D samples with acoustic absorption\n212\tmaps and RT60 labels. The dataset supports evaluator development and validation and informs the construction of\n213\t605-prompt T2AV and 748-prompt I2AV suites, enabling\n214\tsystematic evaluation across diverse acoustic conditions.\n215\t3. We demonstrate that AcoustiTrace diagnostics identify\n216\tconcrete targets for model refinement. A targeted intervention based on a diagnosed range attenuation error\n217\tshows how one such diagnosis can be converted into\n218\tguidance for audio sampling, pointing to broader opportunities for incorporating acoustic principles into training\n219\tobjectives, reward modeling, and candidate selection.\n220\t\n221\tRelated Work\n222\tAudio–Video Generation Models\n223\tAudio generation for video follows two main directions.\n224\tVideo-to-audio methods synthesize a soundtrack for an existing video, with recent diffusion- and flow-based systems\n225\timproving semantic relevance, temporal alignment, and audio quality (Zhang et al. 2026; Cheng et al. 2025; Shan et al.\n226\t2025; Jeong et al. 2025). Joint audio–video models instead\n227\tgenerate both modalities in a shared process, 