ttool impa\n434\twooden\n435\t\n436\tme\n437\ttallic\n438\t\n439\te\n440\tod\n441\two\n442\t\n443\tm\n444\t\n445\trs\n446\trio\n447\tnte ces\n448\ta\n449\tei\n450\tffic r sp\n451\to\n452\t/\n453\tc\n454\too\n455\tbli\n456\tnd\n457\tpu\n458\tri\n459\the\n460\tot\n461\t\n462\tnis\n463\tten\n464\t\n465\tct\n466\t\n467\tpa\n468\t\n469\tact\n470\tImp cay\n471\tDe\n472\t\n473\tother\n474\tmovi\n475\tng\n476\t\n477\tMo\n478\tLou tiondne\n479\tss\n480\t\n481\tLo\n482\tgA\n483\tTim ttac\n484\te k\n485\t\n486\tl\n487\tLatera\n488\tch Stability\n489\toa\n490\tpr n\n491\tAp Gai\n492\t\n493\tles\n494\t\n495\tSound n\n496\tratio\n497\tGene\n498\t\n499\tReal-world audio–video anchors We retrieve candidate\n500\tvideos from public sources using queries tailored to observable acoustic relations, such as different motion strengths,\n501\t\n502\tpassenger cars\n503\t\n504\tGuided by the eight evaluation dimensions, we construct a\n505\tdataset that combines real-world audio–video anchors with\n506\ta dedicated RGB-D collection containing Acoustic Alpha\n507\tMaps and RT60 labels. The dataset supports evaluator development, validation, and prompt design. Further details are\n508\tprovided in the supplementary material.\n509\t\n510\tve\n511\thic\n512\t\n513\tality\n514\tCaus tion\n515\tViola\n516\t\n517\tDataset Construction\n518\t\n519\tra\n520\til\n521\t\n522\tles\n523\t\n524\tAcoustiTrace evaluates acoustic physical realism in generated audio–video content by examining whether the generated soundtrack is consistent with visible events and scene\n525\tconditions. Its test cases are organized around the acoustic process, which comprises three stages: sound generation,\n526\tpropagation environment, and acoustic reception.\n527\tThis organization yields eight evaluation dimensions:\n528\tMotion–Loudness, Log Attack Time, Impact Decay, RT60\n529\tConsistency, Causality Violation, Range Attenuation, Approach Gain, and Lateral Stability. Figure 3 summarizes\n530\tthe prompt suites, which contain 605 T2AV and 748 I2AV\n531\tprompts.\n532\tAs shown in Figure 2, AcoustiTrace links data curation, evaluator validation, and prompt construction: the same\n533\tacoustic relations guide dataset construction, evaluator design, and prompt-suite coverage. This keeps the benchmark\n534\tscope aligned with validated evaluator capabilities.\n535\t\n536\tcars\n537\tracing\n538\ts\n539\ticle\n540\tveh\n541\tncy\n542\trge\n543\tme\n544\t\n545\tBenchmark Design\n546\t\n547\tc\n548\tcy\n549\tor\n550\tot\n551\t\n552\tAcoustiTrace\n553\t\n554\tm\n555\t\n556\tEvaluation has progressed from multidimensional video\n557\tbenchmarks such as VBench to audio–video benchmarks and\n558\tmetrics for perceptual quality, semantic alignment, synchronization, spatial correspondence, and instruction following\n559\t(Huang et al. 2024; Yariv et al. 2024; Shimada et al. 2026;\n560\tHua et al. 2026; Cao et al. 2025). Recent work further targets physical realism: PhyAVBench measures responses to\n561\tcontrolled acoustic changes, FlatSounds tests single-factor\n562\tphysical trends, PAVAS relates estimated impact physics to\n563\tacoustic attributes, and AV-Phys Bench evaluates physical\n564\tconsistency within and across modalities (Xie et al. 2025; Li\n565\tet al. 2026; Oh et al. 2026; Cui et al. 2026). Together, these\n566\tstudies establish important paradigms for evaluating physical sensitivity, trend compliance, and physical commonsense.\n567\tAcoustiTrace complements these efforts by organizing validated acoustic measurements around the acoustic process.\n568\tIt localizes which acoustic relation fails and quantifies the\n569\tcorresponding deviation in interpretable physical terms.\n570\t\n571\tn/e\n572\t\n573\tEvaluation of Video and Audio–Video Generation\n574\t\n575\tsire\n576\t\n577\t2025; Low, Wang, and Katyal 2025; HaCohen et al. 2026;\n578\tSII-OpenMOSS Team et al. 2026; Ji et al. 2026; Liu et al.\n579\t2026b). These systems primarily optimize perceptual quality, semantic coherence, and synchronization; their fidelity\n580\tto the acoustic process implied by the scene remains less\n581\tsystematically characterized.\n582\t\n583\tFigure 3: Distribution of the AcoustiTrace prompt suites. The\n584\tsunburst chart shows their hierarchical composition, with\n585\tsector area proportional to prompt count.\n586\t\n587\tisolated impacts, timed contacts, approaching or receding\n588\tsources, and motion at approximately constant range. Candidates then pass automated multimodal large language model\n589\t(MLLM) and task-specific detector filters, followed by human review to confirm that the target relation is observable and measurable in both modalities. This process yields\n590\t11,296 unique real-world audio–video clips.\n591\tAcoustically annotated RGB-D observations RT60 Consistency assesses whether the reverberation implied by the\n592\tvisible environment agrees with the apparent RT60 estimated\n593\tfrom generated audio. Its visual representation is guided by\n594\tthe Sabine relation, which links reverberation time to room\n595\tvolume and equivalent absorption area (Kinsler et al. 2000):\n596\tT60 = 0.161\n597\t\n598\tV\n599\t,\n600\tA\n601\t\n602\tA=\n603\t\n604\tX\n605\t\n606\tSj αj ,\n607\t\n608\tj\n609\t\n610\twhere V denotes room volume, Sj is the area of boundary\n611\tregion j, and αj is its frequency-dependent absorption coefficient. Under this relation, visual estimation of RT60 requires\n612\tgeometric cues related to V and Sj , together with spatially\n613\tresolved absorption cues related to αj .\n614\tTo our knowledge, no existing large-scale dataset directly\n615\tpairs indoor RGB-D observations with pixel-level surface\n616\tabsorption maps and band-specific RT60 labels. We therefore\n617\tconstruct 82,828 samples, each combining a Matterport3D\n618\tRGB-D observation, a PTB-derived Acoustic Alpha Map,\n619\tand a 500 Hz RT60 label estimated from a SoundSpaces 2.0\n620\troom impulse response (Chang et al. 2017; PhysikalischTechnische Bundesanstalt n.d.; Chen et al. 2022; Schroeder\n621\t1965).\n622\t\n623\t\fthe conditioning image identifies the struck object and its\n624\tmaterial context.\n625\tSound Generation: Impact Decay Impact Decay evaluates whether the sound following an isolated visible impact\n626\texhibits a physically plausible loss of energy. An impact\n627\texcites transient structural vibrations that dissipate through\n628\tmaterial damping and acoustic radiation, often producing\n629\tan approximately exponential decay. The evaluator measures\n630\thow well the post-impact energy envelope follows this general dissipative pattern using the coefficient of determination\n631\tof an exponential fit. Higher fit quality indicates more plausible impact decay.\n632\t\n633\tFigure 4: Construction of the AcoustiTrace dataset from realworld audio–video anchors and acoustically annotated RGBD observations.\n634\t\n635\tAcoustic Evaluation Dimensions\n636\tAcoustiTrace evaluates eight acoustic relations spanning\n637\tsound generation, propagation environment, and acoustic reception. Each dimension specifies the required visual evidence, audio measurement, and expected acoustic relation.\n638\tEach evaluator is applied only when its required visual evidence and corresponding audio event or segment can be reliably localized and measured. Dimension-specific validity\n639\tcriteria, detector settings, and score mappings are provided\n640\tin the supplementary material.\n641\tSound\n642\tGeneration:\n643\tMotion–Loudness Motion–\n644\tLoudness probes the coupling between visible excitation\n645\tstrength and emitted acoustic energy. A stronger action is\n646\texpected to produce a louder event. The evaluator tests\n647\twhether this relation is preserved between actions within the\n648\tsame generated sample. The evaluation first employs two\n649\texpert models for event localization in the visual and audio\n650\tstreams (Zhou et al. 2025; Hai et al. 2025). The localized\n651\tactions are then compared by an MLLM using visual\n652\tevidence only. The MLLM ranks their visible action strength\n653\taccording to motion amplitude, speed, motion range, and\n654\tduration. The evaluator checks whether the visually stronger\n655\taction is paired with the higher audio level.\n656\tSound Generation: Log Attack Time Log Attack Time\n657\tevaluates whether the onset of a generated impact is consistent with the material and contact conditions of a real\n658\treference. For each I2AV test case, the conditioning image\n659\tand reference audio are drawn from the same temporally\n660\taligned Greatest Hits recording, in which a drumstick strikes\n661\ta visible object (Owens et al. 2016). Hard and rigid contacts generally exhibit faster onsets than soft or compliant\n662\tcontacts. We localize the corresponding impacts in the generated and reference audio and compute Log Attack Time\n663\tfor each; a smaller absolute difference indicates closer onset agreement. This dimension is restricted to I2AV because\n664\t\n665\tPropagation Environment: RT60 Consistency RT60\n666\tConsistency evaluates whether the reverberation present in\n667\tthe generated audio agrees with that implied by the visible environment. Specifically, this dimension compares the\n668\tRT60 inferred from visual scene cues with the apparent RT60\n669\testimated from the generated audio. On the visual side, we\n670\tfine-tune an MLLM equipped with a Sabine-guided physics\n671\thead to estimate RT60 from the visible scene. Details of the\n672\tMLLM fine-tuning strategy and the design of the physics\n673\thead are provided in the supplementary material. On the\n674\taudio side, we estimate the apparent RT60 from a noisecompensated Schroeder energy decay curve.\n675\tPropagation Environment: Causality Violation Causality Violation evaluates whether an audible event begins before the visible event that produces it. Using expert models,\n676\twe localize and temporally match the corresponding visual\n677\tand audio events. The detected audio onset is then compared\n678\twith the visible contact time. An audio onset that precedes\n679\tthe visible event is counted as a causality violation.\n680\tAcoustic Reception: Range Attenuation Range Attenuation evaluates whether the generated audio preserves the\n681\texpected distance-dependent attenuation as a visible sound\n682\tsource moves farther from the listener. In an ideal free field\n683\twith approximately constant source power, sound intensity\n684\tfollows the inverse-square law (Kinsler et al. 2000). We track\n685\tthe dominant sound source in the video, combine its spatial trajectory with depth estimates to obtain a relative range\n686\tcurve, and align this curve with the short-time audio level trajectory. The observed relative level change is compared with\n687\tthe distance-attenuation reference derived from the inversesquare law, and the coefficient of determination R2 measures\n688\thow well this reference explains the generated audio level trajectory.\n689\tAcoustic Reception: Approach Gain Approach Gain applies to intervals in which the tracked source moves toward\n690\tthe listener. It evaluates whether decreasing visual range is\n691\taccompanied by a corresponding increase in received audio\n692\tlevel. Flattened, reversed, or temporally inconsistent gain\n693\tpatterns receive lower scores.\n694\tAcoustic Reception: Lateral Stability Lateral Stability\n695\tevaluates the received level when the source remains at approximately constant range or moves primarily across the\n696\tlistener’s field of view. After selecting intervals with limited\n697\tvariation in relative depth, the evaluator measures systematic\n698\t\n699\t\fTask Evaluator\n700\t\n701\tLTX-2.3 JavisDiT++ NAVA Ovi UniVerse-1 MOVA Veo 3.1∗ Seedance 2.0∗ Wan 2.7∗\n702\t\n703\tT2AV Range Attenuation\n704\tApproach Gain\n705\tLateral Stability\n706\tMotion–Loudness\n707\tImpact Decay\n708\tCausality Violation\n709\tRT60 Consistency\n710\t\n711\t81.63\n712\t74.11\n713\t92.18\n714\t88.51\n715\t90.00\n716\t86.52\n717\t41.08\n718\t\n719\t39.17\n720\t68.39\n721\t87.55\n722\t51.52\n723\t73.62\n724\t86.60\n725\t69.81\n726\t\n727\t68.12\n728\t69.88\n729\t84.42\n730\t75.86\n731\t88.59\n732\t93.22\n733\t69.74\n734\t\n735\t57.13\n736\t70.62\n737\t89.56\n738\t56.44\n739\t98.62\n740\t88.89\n741\t69.07\n742\t\n743\t–\n744\t–\n745\t–\n746\t–\n747\t–\n748\t–\n749\t–\n750\t\n751\t62.42\n752\t81.14\n753\t86.80\n754\t74.00\n755\t59.10\n756\t83.33\n757\t53.57\n758\t\n759\t71.85\n760\t88.68\n761\t81.79\n762\t75.10\n763\t98.61\n764\t98.62\n765\t73.51\n766\t\n767\t86.48\n768\t71.32\n769\t76.57\n770\t83.98\n771\t91.39\n772\t99.16\n773\t24.28\n774\t\n775\t74.49\n776\t71.69\n777\t82.03\n778\t82.37\n779\t85.91\n780\t95.68\n781\t33.20\n782\t\n783\tI2AV Range Attenuation\n784\tApproach Gain\n785\tLateral Stability\n786\tMotion–Loudness\n787\tImpact Decay\n788\tCausality Violation\n789\tLog Attack Time\n790\tRT60 Consistency\n791\t\n792\t66.89\n793\t65.22\n794\t91.33\n795\t72.73\n796\t84.95\n797\t88.39\n798\t62.56\n799\t42.12\n800\t\n801\t–\n802\t–\n803\t–\n804\t–\n805\t–\n806\t–\n807\t–\n808\t–\n809\t\n810\t67.78\n811\t65.88\n812\t86.91\n813\t89.55\n814\t83.64\n815\t87.80\n816\t65.29\n817\t70.71\n818\t\n819\t45.30\n820\t71.98\n821\t91.08\n822\t54.33\n823\t83.20\n824\t50.60\n825\t50.42\n826\t66.91\n827\t\n828\t32.53\n829\t65.81\n830\t84.70\n831\t50.77\n832\t63.13\n833\t44.97\n834\t34.65\n835\t42.06\n836\t\n837\t53.50\n838\t78.79\n839\t86.01\n840\t80.90\n841\t84.28\n842\t90.00\n843\t63.88\n844\t55.01\n845\t\n846\t63.70\n847\t85.76\n848\t81.14\n849\t84.53\n850\t83.48\n851\t97.36\n852\t73.28\n853\t46.17\n854\t\n855\t59.98\n856\t70.83\n857\t79.50\n858\t83.92\n859\t87.39\n860\t97.03\n861\t50.15\n862\t17.22\n863\t\n864\t45.17\n865\t76.12\n866\t77.38\n867\t85.67\n868\t72.46\n869\t95.75\n870\t73.70\n871\t76.28\n872\t\n873\tTable 1: AcoustiTrace results for T2AV and I2AV generation. Bold values indicate the best model result for each metric within\n874\teach task. Higher values indicate greater physical consistency. Models marked with ∗ are proprietary systems evaluated through\n875\tofficial interfaces. A dash indicates that the evaluated model release does not natively support the corresponding task.\n876\tlevel drift and short-time loudness variation. Generated audio is considered more physically consistent when its level\n877\tremains stable in the absence of a substantial range change.\n878\t\n879\tBenchmarking Audio–Video Generators\n880\tMain Results\n881\tWe evaluate nine joint audio–video generators: LTX-2.3 (HaCohen et al. 2026), JavisDiT++ (Liu et al. 2026b), NAVA (Ji\n882\tet al. 2026), Ovi (Low, Wang, and Katyal 2025), UniVerse1 (Wang et al. 2025), and MOVA (SII-OpenMOSS Team et al.\n883\t2026), along with Veo 3.1 (Google DeepMind 2026, 2025),\n884\tSeedance 2.0 (Team Seedance et al. 2026), and Wan 2.7 (Alibaba Cloud Community 2026). We use each model’s supported settings. Table 1 reports conditional scores on valid\n885\toutputs. Across the reported model–evaluator cells, validity\n886\tranges from 40.5% to 100% (median 99.8%); coverage is\n887\tconsistently lowest for RT60, with full cell-wise rates and\n888\texclusion criteria provided in the supplementary material.\n889\tAs shown in the T2AV block of Table 1, no model\n890\tdominates across all dimensions. LTX-2.3 leads Motion–\n891\tLoudness and Lateral Stability, Ovi leads Impact Decay,\n892\tVeo 3.1 leads Approach Gain and RT60 Consistency, and\n893\tSeedance 2.0 leads Range Attenuation and Causality Violation. The I2AV results show a different pattern of\n894\tdimension-wise leadership. NAVA leads Range Attenuation\n895\tand Motion–Loudness, LTX-2.3 leads Lateral Stability, Veo\n896\t3.1 leads Approach Gain and Causality Violation, Seedance\n897\t2.0 leads Impact Decay, and Wan 2.7 leads Log Attack Time\n898\tand RT60 Consistency.\n899\tMechanism-level patterns and design implications\n900\tAcross T2AV and I2AV, the results reveal a consistent divide\n901\tbetween acoustic relations aligned with current design priorities and those requiring sustained modeling of geometric\n902\tor environmental structure. Although implemented through\n903\t\n904\tdifferent mechanisms, including temporal alignment, multimodal fusion, joint latent generation, and the composition of\n905\tseparate modality representations, current generators largely\n906\tprioritize semantic and temporal alignment. This shared emphasis may help explain why most models achieve high consistency scores under the Causality Violation evaluator.\n907\tAcoustic relations that are locally explicit or qualitatively\n908\tobservable within short training clips, such as the relation\n909\tbetween visible action magnitude and sound level, decay\n910\tfollowing an isolated impact, loudness stability at approximately constant range, and the loudness increase during approach, may be easier to learn than relations that depend on\n911\tspatial or environmental consistency over longer intervals.\n912\tThis may explain the strong Motion–Loudness and Impact\n913\tDecay performance of several models, together with the generally robust Lateral Stability and Approach Gain results.\n914\tBy contrast, Log Attack Time, RT60 Consistency, and\n915\tRange Attenuation remain particularly challenging. Log Attack Time remains lower than Impact Decay for eight of\n916\tthe nine models. This gap likely reflects two limitations in\n917\tcurrent training and evaluation practice. First, general A/V\n918\ttraining data rarely provide explicit supervision linking material and contact conditions to detailed sound onset dynamics. Second, commonly used semantic alignment and synchronization metrics, including embedding measures such\n919\tas ImageBind (Girdhar et al. 2023), emphasize overall correspondence but are less sensitive to local envelope structure. Because these metrics shape model selection and optimization priorities, current systems receive limited pressure\n920\tto improve such detailed acoustic properties. Consequently,\n921\tmodels may learn that an impact sound should occur without\n922\treliably learning how quickly it should rise.\n923\tFor RT60 Consistency, learning physically grounded scene\n924\tacoustics requires training examples with observable reverberation decay, alongside reliable information on room geometry and materials. Because such clean and informative\n925\t\n926\t\f(a) AcoustiTrace-guided audio denoising\n927\tGenerated\n928\tTrajectory\n929\tEvaluator\n930\t\n931\tAudio\n932\tGenerated Audio\n933\tEnvelope 𝑬𝜽\n934\t\n935\t𝑥𝑇−1 −∇𝑥 𝐿𝑑𝑖𝑠𝑡\n936\t𝑇\n937\t\n938\tExpected Audio\n939\tEnvelope 𝑬∗\n940\t\n941\t𝑬𝜽\n942\t𝔼(𝑖,𝑗)∈𝒫 [∆𝑖𝑗 log ]2\n943\t𝑬∗\n944\t\n945\tUpdate\n946\t\n947\tDepth\n948\tBefore guidance\n949\tLog-mel\n950\t(spectrogram)\n951\t\n952\tOriginal\n953\tExpected\n954\t\n955\tCanonical\n956\t0.8\n957\t\n958\t𝑥𝑇−1\n959\t\n960\t𝑥𝑇−2\n961\t\n962\tAudio denoiser\n963\t\n964\t…\n965\t\n966\t𝑥0\n967\t\n968\tOriginal\n969\tExpected\n970\t\n971\t(c) Aggregate improvement\n972\t1.0\n973\t\n974\t𝑥𝑇\n975\t\n976\tAfter guidance\n977\t\n978\tLog-mel\n979\t(spectrogram)\n980\t\n981\tEnvelope(dB)\n982\t\n983\tVideo\n984\t\n985\tRGB\n986\t\n987\tEnvelope(dB)\n988\t\n989\tA/V Generator\n990\t\n991\t(b) Qualitative example: receding source\n992\t\n993\tNo guidance\n994\t\n995\tSample-level evidence\n996\tOurs\n997\t\n998\tRelative 𝑅2 Gain\n999\t\n1000\t𝟎. 𝟖𝟔\n1001\t\n1002\t𝟎. 𝟖𝟐\n1003\t𝟎. 𝟕𝟏\n1004\t\n1005\t0.6\n1006\t\n1007\t𝟎. 𝟒𝟓\n1008\t\n1009\t0.2\n1010\t0.0\n1011\t\n1012\t𝟎. 𝟒𝟑\n1013\t𝟎. 𝟑𝟓\n1014\t\n1015\t𝟎. 𝟑𝟒\n1016\t\n1017\t0.4\n1018\t\n1019\t|Spearman|\n1020\t\n1021\t126\n1022\t\n1023\t4.95×\n1024\t\n1025\tvs. no guidance\n1026\t\n1027\tvs. no guidance\n1028\t\n1029\tTotal samples\n1030\t\n1031\t+5.10%\n1032\t\n1033\t1.28×\n1034\t\n1035\tvs. canonical\n1036\tvs. canonical\n1037\tWe keep the video fixed and resample only the audio,\n1038\twithout training or parameter updates.\n1039\t\n1040\t𝟎. 𝟑𝟎\n1041\t\n1042\t𝟎. 𝟎𝟗\n1043\t\n1044\t𝑅2\n1045\t\n1046\tMean |Spearman|\n1047\t\n1048\t+20.2%\n1049\t\n1050\t|Slope|\n1051\t\n1052\tFigure 5: From diagnosis to intervention. (a) The Range Attenuation residual from a fixed visual trajectory guides decoded-mel\n1053\taudio denoising. (b) A qualitative receding-source example visualizes how guidance reshapes the audio envelope toward the\n1054\texpected attenuation trend. (c) Aggregate results show improved Range Attenuation R2 and stronger inverse range–loudness\n1055\tcoupling over unguided audio resampling.\n1056\tdata are scarce, current models may not reliably capture how\n1057\tvisible room properties shape reverberation.\n1058\tRange Attenuation reveals a related but distinct capability gap. The comparatively strong Approach Gain results\n1059\tsuggest that current models have learned a coarse association between source distance and loudness, but this implicit\n1060\trelation does not consistently extend to the attenuation expected as distance changes. This gap likely reflects limited\n1061\tsupervision over source trajectories, emission strength, and\n1062\tcorresponding loudness curves.\n1063\tTaken together, these results indicate that producing plausible sound events does not necessarily imply faithful modeling of the underlying acoustic mechanisms. These findings\n1064\thighlight an urgent need for curated training data annotated\n1065\twith relevant physical quantities, together with objectives\n1066\tthat explicitly supervise the relations among them.\n1067\t\n1068\tRange-Guided Audio Sampling\n1069\tBuilding on these insights, we conduct an initial study to test\n1070\twhether AcoustiTrace diagnostics can directly guide model\n1071\trefinement. We target the identified Range Attenuation failure and formulate the expected attenuation relation used by\n1072\tthe evaluator as a differentiable guidance objective for audio\n1073\tsampling.\n1074\tOur controlled intervention on LTX-2.3 holds the generated video fixed and resamples only the audio stream under\n1075\tbaseline and guided conditions. From the fixed video, we estimate source ranges dt and guide a differentiable decoded-mel\n1076\tamplitude proxy et via\n1077\t\u00152\n1078\tX\u0014\n1079\tdj + ϵ\n1080\tei + ϵ\n1081\t− γ log\n1082\t.\n1083\tLdist =\n1084\tlog\n1085\tej + ϵ\n1086\tdi + ϵ\n1087\ti,j\n1088\tWe set γ = 1 to target inverse-distance amplitude attenuation. A held-out calibration confirmed that the proxy pre-\n1089\t\n1090\tMetric\n1091\t2\n1092\t\n1093\tMean R\n1094\tSpearman ρ\n1095\tCLAP\n1096\tLUFS\n1097\tAudiobox PQ\n1098\t\n1099\tCanonical\n1100\t\n1101\tNo guidance\n1102\t\n1103\tGuided\n1104\t\n1105\t0.8163\n1106\t−0.3411\n1107\t0.1029\n1108\t−23.601\n1109\t6.031\n1110\t\n1111\t0.7138\n1112\t−0.0880\n1113\t0.0498\n1114\t−23.039\n1115\t6.106\n1116\t\n1117\t0.8580\n1118\t−0.4356\n1119\t0.1049\n1120\t−23.586\n1121\t5.884\n1122\t\n1123\tTable 2: Range-guided audio sampling evaluated using target\n1124\tattenuation metrics and non-target quality metrics, with the\n1125\tcanonical video stream held fixed. No-guidance and guided\n1126\tconditions resample only the audio.\n1127\t\n1128\tserves injected 1/r waveform scaling (γ̂ = 0.997). The objective encourages relative audio levels to follow the visual\n1129\trange trajectory. Further implementation and calibration details are provided in the supplementary material.\n1130\tAs summarized in Table 2 and Figure 5(c), audio-only resampling without guidance reduces the mean Range Attenuation R2 from 0.8163 for the canonical outputs to 0.7138.\n1131\tThis sensitivity to audio resampling reveals instability in the\n1132\tdefault sampling process, which does not reliably reproduce\n1133\tthe distance-dependent attenuation expected from acoustic\n1134\tpropagation.\n1135\tDistance guidance increases the mean Range Attenuation\n1136\tR2 from 0.7138 to 0.8580 and outperforms unguided resampling in 80.16% of cases. The mean Spearman correlation\n1137\tshifts from −0.0880 to −0.4356, indicating a stronger inverse\n1138\trelation between visual range and audio level. The qualitative example in Figure 5(b) shows that guidance yields an\n1139\tenvelope that more closely follows the expected attenuation\n1140\ttrajectory.\n1141\tWe assess non-target effects using CLAP similar-\n1142\t\n1143\t\f(a) Evaluator Sensitivity to Physical Perturbations\n1144\t\n1145\tScore (0-100)\n1146\t\n1147\t100\n1148\t\n1149\tLog Attack Time\n1150\t\n1151\tImpact Decay\n1152\t\n1153\t(b) Human–Evaluator Agreement\n1154\tRT60 Consistency\n1155\t\n1156\t50\n1157\t\n1158\t0\n1159\t0\n1160\t3\n1161\t6\n1162\t9\n1163\t12\n1164\tStronger-event attenuation (dB)\n1165\t\n1166\t1 1.5 2\n1167\t4\n1168\tAttack-duration scaling factor (×)\n1169\t\n1170\t0\n1171\t0.25 0.5 0.75\n1172\t1\n1173\tDecay-tail perturbation strength α\n1174\t\n1175\tCausality Violation\n1176\t\n1177\tRange Attenuation\n1178\t\n1179\tApproach Gain\n1180\t\n1181\tScore (0-100)\n1182\t\n1183\t100\n1184\t\n1185\t0.5\n1186\t1 1.5\n1187\t2\n1188\t3\n1189\tTarget 𝑇60audio / 𝑇60visual ratio\n1190\t\n1191\tLateral Stability\n1192\t\n1193\t50\n1194\t\n1195\t0\n1196\t\n1197\t0\n1198\t\n1199\t20 40\n1200\t80\n1201\t120\n1202\tInjected audio lead (ms)\n1203\t\n1204\t0\n1205\t0.25 0.5 0.75\n1206\t1\n1207\tEnvelope-flattening strength λ\n1208\t\n1209\t0\n1210\t0.25 0.5 0.75\n1211\t1\n1212\tEnvelope-flattening strength λ\n1213\t\n1214\t0\n1215\t2\n1216\t4\n1217\t6\n1218\t8\n1219\tInjected fluctuation amplitude (dB)\n1220\t\n1221\tMotion-Loudness\n1222\tLog Attack Time\n1223\tImpact Decay\n1224\tRT60 Consistency\n1225\tCausality Violation\n1226\tRange Attenuation\n1227\tApproach Gain\n1228\tLateral Stability\n1229\t\n1230\t60% 70% 80% 90%\n1231\t87.9%\n1232\t93.5%\n1233\t89.5%\n1234\t\n1235\t88.7%\n1236\t93.5%\n1237\t\n1238\t91.1%\n1239\t90.3%\n1240\t92.7%\n1241\t\n1242\t(c) Comparison with PhyAVBench CPRS\n1243\tAcoustiTrace Range Attenuation\n1244\t\n1245\tMotion-Loudness\n1246\t\n1247\t100\n1248\t\n1249\tLTX-2.3\n1250\tNAVA\n1251\tJavisDiT++\n1252\t\n1253\t50\n1254\t\n1255\t0\n1256\t\n1257\tN = 300 pairs\n1258\t(100 per model)\n1259\tPearson r: −0.134\n1260\tSpearman ρ: −0.142\n1261\t\n1262\t0.5\n1263\t1.0\n1264\tPhyAVBench CPRS (LAION-CLAP)\n1265\t\n1266\tFigure 6: (a) Responses of the eight evaluators to controlled audio perturbations that selectively violate their targeted acoustic\n1267\trelations. The unperturbed point in each sweep corresponds to the original, unmodified real-world A/V samples. For RT60\n1268\tConsistency, perturbation severity increases as the audio-to-visual RT60 ratio deviates from 1. (b) Alignment between evaluator\n1269\tpreferences and human judgments on paired generated A/V samples. Error bars denote 95% confidence intervals. (c) AcoustiTrace\n1270\tRange Attenuation scores versus PhyAVBench CPRS using LAION-CLAP on 300 pairs from LTX-2.3, NAVA, and JavisDiT++.\n1271\tity (Elizalde et al. 2023), integrated LUFS (International\n1272\tTelecommunication Union 2023; European Broadcasting\n1273\tUnion 2023), and the Production Quality (PQ) score from\n1274\tMeta Audiobox Aesthetics (Tjandra et al. 2025) on the same\n1275\tfixed-video pairs. Distance guidance increases CLAP similarity from 0.0498 to 0.1049. Integrated loudness remains\n1276\tnearly identical to the canonical outputs. Audiobox Aesthetics PQ decreases slightly but remains close to the canonical\n1277\tscore of 6.0306. Overall, the intervention improves the targeted attenuation relation and text–audio alignment without\n1278\trelying on global loudness adjustment, while largely preserving predicted production quality.\n1279\tThis intervention demonstrates a concrete path from diagnostic measurement to model refinement. AcoustiTrace identifies a failed acoustic relation, quantifies the corresponding\n1280\tdeviation, and converts the diagnosis into an executable objective for generation. More broadly, the acoustic relations\n1281\trevealed by our benchmark can inform future training objectives, reward models, and candidate-selection strategies.\n1282\t\n1283\tEvaluator Validation and Human Alignment\n1284\tWe assess the reliability of AcoustiTrace through complementary validation experiments covering real-world relation recovery, controlled perturbation sensitivity, and human\n1285\talignment on generated A/V content. We first test all evaluators on our collected real-world A/V dataset to determine\n1286\twhether they recover the expected acoustic relations, with\n1287\tthe original samples represented by the unperturbed points\n1288\tin Figure 6(a). We then apply controlled audio perturbations\n1289\tto selectively violate the acoustic relation targeted by each\n1290\tevaluator. As shown in Figure 6(a), all evaluators respond\n1291\tin the expected direction as the targeted violation becomes\n1292\tmore severe.\n1293\tWe also compare evaluator preferences with human judg-\n1294\t\n1295\tments. A panel of 30 expert raters provides more than\n1296\t1,000 judgments on paired generated A/V samples, indicating which sample better satisfies the target acoustic relation.\n1297\tThe high agreement shown in Figure 6(b) indicates strong\n1298\talignment between evaluator preferences and human judgments. Detailed validation protocols and quantitative results\n1299\tare provided in the supplementary material.\n1300\t\n1301\tComparison with PhyAVBench CPRS\n1302\tWe further compare AcoustiTrace with PhyAVBench’s official LAION-CLAP CPRS on its inverse-square-law near–far\n1303\tprompt pairs. Across 300 valid pairs generated by LTX-2.3,\n1304\tNAVA, and JavisDiT++, CPRS and the AcoustiTrace Range\n1305\tAttenuation score exhibit only weak correlation. CPRS measures whether the near-to-far embedding transition follows\n1306\tthe official reference direction, whereas AcoustiTrace evaluates whether the visually estimated distance change between the near and far samples is accompanied by the corresponding audio attenuation. As shown in Figure 6(c), the\n1307\tresults include both high-CPRS/low-AcoustiTrace and lowCPRS/high-AcoustiTrace cases, indicating that embeddingtransition sensitivity and relation-specific acoustic diagnosis\n1308\tcapture complementary properties. Detailed protocols and\n1309\tcase analyses are provided in the supplementary material.\n1310\t\n1311\tConclusion\n1312\tWe present AcoustiTrace, a diagnostic benchmark for evaluating acoustic physical realism in T2AV and I2AV generation.\n1313\tFeaturing eight dimensions organized around the acoustic\n1314\tprocess and grounded in validated and interpretable acoustic\n1315\tmeasurements, AcoustiTrace reveals that plausible and synchronized sound events do not guarantee faithful modeling of\n1316\tthe tested acoustic relations. A targeted intervention further\n1317\tdemonstrates that diagnostic evidence from AcoustiTrace can\n1318\t\n1319\t\fguide model refinement, providing a basis for incorporating\n1320\tacoustic principles into training objectives, reward modeling,\n1321\tand candidate selection.\n1322\t\n1323\tReferences\n1324\tAlibaba Cloud Community. 2026.\n1325\tAlibaba Unveils\n1326\tWan2.7-Video to Elevate Creators from Executors to\n1327\tDirectors.\n1328\thttps://www.alibabacloud.com/blog/alibabaunveils-wan2-7-video-to-elevate-creators-from-executorsto-directors_603009. Accessed: 2026-07-26.\n1329\tCao, Z.; Wang, T.; Wang, J.; Wang, Y.; Zhang, Y.; Wang, J.;\n1330\tChen, J.; Deng, M.; Guo, Y.; Liao, C.; Zhang, Y.; Zhang, Z.;\n1331\tand Liu, J. 2025. T2AV-Compass: Towards Unified Evaluation for Text-to-Audio-Video Generation. Accepted at the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML 2026),\n1332\tarXiv:2512.21094.\n1333\tChang, A. X.; Dai, A.; Funkhouser, T.; Halber, M.; Niessner, M.; Savva, M.; Song, S.; Zeng, A.; and Zhang, Y. 2017.\n1334\tMatterport3D: Learning from RGB-D Data in Indoor Environments. In 2017 International Conference on 3D Vision,\n1335\t667–676.\n1336\tChen, C.; Schissler, C.; Garg, S.; Kobernik, P.; Clegg, A.;\n1337\tCalamia, P.; Batra, D.; Robinson, P.; and Grauman, K. 2022.\n1338\tSoundSpaces 2.0: A Simulation Platform for Visual-Acoustic\n1339\tLearning. In Advances in Neural Information Processing\n1340\tSystems, volume 35, 8896–8911.\n1341\tCheng, H. K.; Ishii, M.; Hayakawa, A.; Shibuya, T.; Schwing,\n1342\tA.; and Mitsufuji, Y. 2025. MMAudio: Taming Multimodal\n1343\tJoint Training for High-Quality Video-to-Audio Synthesis.\n1344\tIn Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer\n1345\tVision and Pattern Recognition, 28901–28911.\n1346\tCui, Z.; Liu, X.; Fang, H.; Xu, M.; Liu, J.; Xu, Z.; Pian,\n1347\tW.; Deng, S.; Du, F.; Ge, C.; and Tian, Y. 2026. Do\n1348\tJoint Audio-Video Generation Models Understand Physics?\n1349\tarXiv:2605.07061.\n1350\tElizalde, B.; Deshmukh, S.; Al Ismail, M.; and Wang, H.\n1351\t2023. CLAP: Learning Audio Concepts from Natural Language Supervision. In 2023 IEEE International Conference\n1352\ton Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP), 1–5.\n1353\tIEEE.\n1354\tEuropean Broadcasting Union. 2023. Loudness Normalisation and Permitted Maximum Level of Audio Signals.\n1355\tEBU Recommendation R 128, Version 5.0, European Broadcasting Union. Available at https://tech.ebu.ch/publications/\n1356\tr128; accessed: 2026-07-29.\n1357\tGirdhar, R.; El-Nouby, A.; Liu, Z.; Singh, M.; Alwala, K. V.;\n1358\tJoulin, A.; and Misra, I. 2023. ImageBind: One Embedding\n1359\tSpace to Bind Them All. In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF\n1360\tConference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition,\n1361\t15180–15190.\n1362\tGoogle DeepMind. 2025. Veo: A Text-to-Video Generation System. https://storage.googleapis.com/deepmindmedia/veo/Veo-3-Tech-Report.pdf. Technical report; accessed: 2026-07-29.\n1363\tGoogle DeepMind. 2026.\n1364\tVeo 3 Model Card.\n1365\thttps://storage.googleapis.com/deepmind-media/Model-\n1366\t\n1367\tCards/Veo-3-Model-Card.pdf. Published May 23, 2025;\n1368\tupdated January 13, 2026; accessed: 2026-07-29.\n1369\tHaCohen, Y.