{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a489d3dcf2baad7f", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1500\t4\n1501\tBeihang University\n1502\t5\n1503\tSchool of Engineering, Westlake University\n1504\t6\n1505\tZhejiang Lab\n1506\t12521030@zju.edu.cn\n1507\t2\n1508\t\n1509\tAbstract\n1510\tThis supplementary document clarifies the diagnostic abstraction and positioning of AcoustiTrace and provides additional\n1511\tdetails on dataset construction, evaluator operating domains,\n1512\tbenchmark validity, evaluator validation, comparison with\n1513\tPhyAVBench CPRS, and range-guided audio sampling.\n1514\t\n1515\tS1\n1516\t\n1517\tDiagnostic Abstraction and Positioning\n1518\t\n1519\tAcoustiTrace treats each evaluation dimension as a relationlevel diagnostic contract rather than as an interchangeable\n1520\toutput-quality score. For dimension k, we write this contract\n1521\tas\n1522\t\u0001\n1523\tDk = Vk , Ak , Rk , Gk , Sk ,\n1524\t(S1)\n1525\twhere Vk extracts the required visual evidence, Ak measures\n1526\tthe corresponding audio quantity, Rk specifies the expected\n1527\tacoustic relation, Gk determines whether that relation is measurable in the generated output, and Sk maps the relation\n1528\tresidual to an interpretable diagnostic score. Learned detectors, trackers, depth estimators, and MLLMs implement\n1529\tparts of the evidence-extraction pipeline; the diagnostic target is defined by the acoustic relation, validity conditions,\n1530\tand residual that connect the two modalities.\n1531\tA central feature of this formulation is that the expected\n1532\trelation is conditioned on evidence in the generated video\n1533\twhenever applicable, rather than assuming that the model\n1534\tfaithfully rendered the requested event, trajectory, or environment. If the evidence required by Gk is absent or cannot\n1535\tbe measured reliably, the output is marked invalid for that dimension. If it is present, the evaluator quantifies the deviation\n1536\tbetween the visual implication and the audio measurement.\n1537\tThis separates observability or prompt-compliance failures\n1538\tfrom violations of an acoustic relation.\n1539\tThe shared contract gives the benchmark four complementary properties. Generated-output conditioning ties evaluation to what the model actually rendered in both modalities. Mechanism localization places each diagnosed relation\n1540\twithin sound generation, propagation environment, or acoustic reception. Validity-aware measurement reports coverage separately from conditional physical-consistency scores.\n1541\tMeasurement-to-intervention exposes relation residuals\n1542\t\n1543\tthat can serve as concrete optimization targets, as demonstrated by the range-guided sampling study. Table S1 summarizes how all eight dimensions instantiate this abstraction.\n1544\tThis diagnostic unit complements existing evaluation\n1545\tparadigms. General audio–video benchmarks such as\n1546\tVABench cover broad perceptual, semantic, temporal, and\n1547\tcontent criteria (Hua et al. 2026). Table S2 instead focuses\n1548\ton the physics-oriented benchmarks most closely related to\n1549\tAcoustiTrace and compares their task coverage and primary\n1550\tdiagnostic capabilities. The entries describe the protocols reported by the respective papers rather than a ranking of their\n1551\toverall scope or quality.\n1552\tThe three task-coverage columns indicate whether a benchmark’s primary protocol directly evaluates text-to-audio–\n1553\tvideo (T2AV), image-to-audio–video (I2AV), or video-toaudio (V2A) systems. “Generated-video reference” means\n1554\tthat the expected physical relation is inferred from visual\n1555\tevidence in the generated video, rather than specified only\n1556\tby the prompt, an input video, or a real-world reference.\n1557\t“Acoustic quantities” indicates that evaluation is explicitly\n1558\tgrounded in measurable descriptors such as level, onset, decay, or reverberation, rather than embedding similarity or\n1559\tgeneric rubric judgments alone. “Per-output diagnosis” requires a relation-specific score or violation signal for an individual generated output, rather than only a pairwise direction\n1560\tor a dataset-level statistic. “Validity handling” requires an explicit applicability gate that separates outputs lacking the evidence needed for measurement from valid outputs with low\n1561\tphysical-consistency scores. “Process-stage structure” indicates that the evaluated relations are organized across multiple stages of the acoustic process rather than presented as\n1562\tan undifferentiated list. Finally, “metric-guided refinement”\n1563\trequires the diagnostic quantity or residual reported during\n1564\tevaluation to be reused as a generation or optimization objective; incorporating physical conditioning without closing\n1565\tthis diagnostic loop is marked as partial support.\n1566\tAcoustiTrace does not replace these complementary\n1567\tparadigms. Its specific emphasis is the combination of\n1568\tgenerated-output conditioning, mechanism-level organization, validity-aware scoring, and explicit per-output relation\n1569\tdiagnosis. These properties allow the same diagnostic resid-\n1570\t\n1571\t\fAcoustic stage\n1572\t\n1573\tDimension\n1574\t\n1575\tVisual evidence\n1576\t\n1577\tAudio quantity\n1578\t\n1579\tDiagnosed relation\n1580\t\n1581\tGeneration\n1582\t\n1583\tMotion–Loudness\n1584\t\n1585\tLocalized action\n1586\tstrength\n1587\t\n1588\tMatched event level\n1589\t\n1590\tGeneration\n1591\t\n1592\tLog Attack Time\n1593\t\n1594\tObject, material, and\n1595\tcontact evidence\n1596\t\n1597\tAttack duration\n1598\t\n1599\tGeneration\n1600\t\n1601\tImpact Decay\n1602\t\n1603\tIsolated visible impact\n1604\t\n1605\tPost-impact decay\n1606\tenvelope\n1607\t\n1608\tPropagation\n1609\t\n1610\tRT60 Consistency\n1611\t\n1612\tScene geometry and\n1613\tmaterial cues\n1614\t\n1615\tApparent 500 Hz\n1616\tRT60\n1617\t\n1618\tPropagation\n1619\t\n1620\tVisible event time\n1621\t\n1622\tAudio-event onset\n1623\t\n1624\tReception\n1625\t\n1626\tCausality\n1627\tViolation\n1628\tRange Attenuation\n1629\t\n1630\tSource-range trajectory\n1631\t\n1632\tReception\n1633\t\n1634\tApproach Gain\n1635\t\n1636\tDecreasing source\n1637\trange\n1638\t\n1639\tShort-time audio\n1640\tlevel\n1641\tShort-time audio\n1642\tlevel\n1643\t\n1644\tReception\n1645\t\n1646\tLateral Stability\n1647\t\n1648\tApproximately constant\n1649\trange\n1650\t\n1651\tLevel drift and\n1652\tfluctuation\n1653\t\n1654\tStronger visible excitation\n1655\tshould emit greater acoustic\n1656\tenergy.\n1657\tOnset dynamics should agree\n1658\twith the visible contact\n1659\tcondition.\n1660\tImpact energy should exhibit a\n1661\tphysically plausible loss\n1662\tpattern.\n1663\tAudio reverberation should\n1664\tagree with the visible\n1665\tenvironment.\n1666\tAn audible effect should not\n1667\tprecede its visible cause.\n1668\tLevel change should follow\n1669\tdistance-dependent attenuation.\n1670\tApproach toward the listener\n1671\tshould produce a\n1672\tcorresponding gain.\n1673\tLevel should remain stable\n1674\twithout a substantial range\n1675\tchange.\n1676\t\n1677\tTable S1: The common diagnostic contract instantiated by the eight AcoustiTrace dimensions. Each dimension links evidence in\n1678\tthe generated video to a measurable audio quantity through an explicit acoustic relation and a dimension-specific validity gate.\n1679\tual used for evaluation to be instantiated as a generation\n1680\tobjective in the range-guided study.\n1681\tThe remainder of this supplement documents the evidence\n1682\tsupporting this diagnostic contract. Real-world anchors establish relation recovery, controlled perturbations test directional sensitivity, human judgments assess preference alignment, the CPRS comparison tests metric non-redundancy,\n1683\tand the range-guided study illustrates how a diagnosed residual can be converted into an executable generation objective.\n1684\t\n1685\tS2\n1686\t\n1687\tDataset Construction Details\n1688\t\n1689\tAcoustiTrace combines real-world audio–video anchors with\n1690\tan acoustically annotated RGB-D collection. The former support prompt construction and evaluator validation under observable acoustic relations, while the latter provide spatially\n1691\tresolved absorption cues and RT60 supervision for the visual\n1692\treverberation estimator.\n1693\t\n1694\tS2.1\n1695\t\n1696\tReal-World Audio–Video Anchors\n1697\t\n1698\tWe retrieve candidate videos from public sources using\n1699\tqueries tailored to the eight evaluation dimensions. The\n1700\tqueries target different visible motion strengths, isolated\n1701\timpacts, timed contacts, approaching or receding sources,\n1702\tand motion at approximately constant range. For Motion–\n1703\tLoudness, each case targets two visually distinguishable action segments with different strengths. Using two actions limits prompt-realization failures and ambiguity in event correspondence. Impact Decay and Causality Violation use eventlevel clips centered on a single visible contact or collision.\n1704\tRange Attenuation, Approach Gain, and Lateral Stability use\n1705\t\n1706\tlonger clips with a trackable source and a clear increasing,\n1707\tdecreasing, or approximately constant-range trajectory.\n1708\tRetrieval uses two task-oriented workflows. Receiver/observer queries cover cars, motorcycles, trains, and\n1709\tairplanes with approaching, receding, pass-by, and fly-over\n1710\tmodifiers. Source-mechanics and causality queries cover\n1711\tobject drops, ball bounces, hammer and material impacts,\n1712\tknocking, clapping, drum hits, breaking events, and everyday clicks or closures. Curated seed categories additionally\n1713\tcover vehicle and aircraft pass-bys, emergency sirens, drones\n1714\tor remote-controlled vehicles, woodworking and metalworking, keyboard and switch clicks, door mechanisms, sports\n1715\timpacts, and human percussive events.\n1716\tCandidate clips undergo automatic screening followed by\n1717\thuman review. Media are standardized to 480p video and 16\n1718\tkHz mono audio and processed in 30 s chunks. Metadata\n1719\tfilters remove sound-effect libraries, music, remixes, compilations, and other unsuitable content; signal gates require at\n1720\tleast 3 dB RMS dynamic range and no more than 80% silence. A Qwen-family MLLM then checks source visibility,\n1721\taudio–visual consistency, relative motion, event structure,\n1722\tcamera motion, and common contamination. Depending on\n1723\tthe target dimension, the remaining automatic stage combines open-vocabulary visual event localization, audio event\n1724\tdetection, source tracking, depth/applicability checks, and\n1725\tsignal-quality measurements. Source-event candidates use\n1726\tOV-AVEL (Zhou et al. 2025) and FlexSED (Hai et al. 2025),\n1727\twith visual and audio events associated within 0.35 s.\n1728\tEach retained clip is reviewed by two reviewers and kept\n1729\tonly when both agree that the target relation is observable\n1730\t\n1731\t\fTask coverage\n1732\tBenchmark\n1733\t\n1734\tDiagnostic capabilities\n1735\t\n1736\tRefinement\n1737\t\n1738\tGenerated-video Acoustic Per-output Validity Process-stage Metric-guided\n1739\tT2AV I2AV V2A\n1740\treference\n1741\tquantities diagnosis handling structure\n1742\trefinement\n1743\t\n1744\tPhyAVBench (Xie et al. 2025)\n1745\tFlatSounds (Li et al. 2026)\n1746\tAV-Phys Bench (Cui et al. 2026)\n1747\tPAVAS / VGG-Impact (Oh et al. 2026)\n1748\t\n1749\t✓\n1750\t×\n1751\t✓\n1752\t×\n1753\t\n1754\t✓\n1755\t×\n1756\t×\n1757\t×\n1758\t\n1759\t✓\n1760\t✓\n1761\t×\n1762\t✓\n1763\t\n1764\t×\n1765\t×\n1766\t✓\n1767\t×\n1768\t\n1769\t×\n1770\t✓\n1771\t✓\n1772\t✓\n1773\t\n1774\t×\n1775\t△\n1776\t×\n1777\t×\n1778\t\n1779\t×\n1780\t×\n1781\t×\n1782\t×\n1783\t\n1784\t✓\n1785\t△\n1786\t△\n1787\t×\n1788\t\n1789\t×\n1790\t×\n1791\t×\n1792\t△\n1793\t\n1794\tAcoustiTrace\n1795\t\n1796\t✓\n1797\t\n1798\t✓\n1799\t\n1800\t×\n1801\t\n1802\t✓\n1803\t\n1804\t✓\n1805\t\n1806\t✓\n1807\t\n1808\t✓\n1809\t\n1810\t✓\n1811\t\n1812\t✓\n1813\t\n1814\tTable S2: Capability matrix for representative physics-oriented audio–video benchmarks. ✓ denotes direct support in the primary\n1815\tprotocol, △ a related but partial capability or a different aggregation level, and × that the capability is not part of the primary\n1816\tprotocol.\n1817\tin the video and that the corresponding audio event or segment can be localized and measured. Clips with ambiguous\n1818\tcontacts, unstable trajectories, competing sound sources, intrusive background music, excessive noise, or other attribution confounds are excluded. The frozen collection contains\n1819\t11,296 unique audio–video clips.\n1820\tRole of the anchors and release scope The real-world\n1821\tanchors intentionally support both prompt construction and\n1822\tevaluator validation. This alignment ensures that the evaluators’ declared operating domains cover the acoustic relations instantiated by the prompt suites. Relation-recovery\n1823\tand controlled-perturbation experiments therefore test measurability, sensitivity, and monotonicity within that operating\n1824\tdomain; they are not claims of open-domain generalization,\n1825\tand the anchors are not generated samples used to rank the\n1826\tevaluated models. For the public-video component, the release manifest is limited to source video identifiers or URLs,\n1827\ttime spans, task labels, and derived annotations or scores;\n1828\traw public-video media are not redistributed.\n1829\t\n1830\tS2.2\n1831\t\n1832\tAcoustically Annotated RGB-D\n1833\tObservations\n1834\t\n1835\tThe visual RT60 estimator is structured by the Sabine relation\n1836\t(Kinsler et al. 2000),\n1837\tX\n1838\tV\n1839\tT60 = 0.161 ,\n1840\tA=\n1841\tSj αj ,\n1842\t(S2)\n1843\tA\n1844\tj\n1845\twhere V is room volume, Sj is the area of boundary region\n1846\tj, and αj is its frequency-dependent absorption coefficient.\n1847\tEquation S2 motivates a representation that combines geometric information with spatially resolved material absorption.\n1848\tWe first obtain RGB-D observations from Matterport3D\n1849\t(Chang et al. 2017). GroundSAM segments visible surfaces\n1850\tand objects, and a Qwen-family MLLM refines each coarse\n1851\tcategory into a material description suitable for acoustic\n1852\tdatabase retrieval. A SentenceTransformer representation\n1853\tmatches that description to its top-1 PTB Room Acoustics\n1854\tAbsorption Coefficient Database entry by cosine similarity (Physikalisch-Technische Bundesanstalt n.d.). We then\n1855\tproject the matched 500 Hz absorption coefficient onto\n1856\tthe corresponding mask; a missing 500 Hz coefficient in\n1857\t\n1858\ta matched PTB row is assigned 0.02. Combining all regions\n1859\tproduces a pixel-level Acoustic Alpha Map, which serves as\n1860\ta third visual modality alongside RGB and depth. All final\n1861\tobservations use this semantic-material matching path; no\n1862\tHSV proxy or fallback maps are included.\n1863\tThe 82,828 observations are split by sample-level stratification over 500 Hz RT60 bins into 57,977 training, 8,285\n1864\tvalidation, and 16,566 test samples. The split is not scenedisjoint: all three subsets contain observations from the same\n1865\t83 Matterport3D scenes. It is used for in-domain estimator development and operating-domain coverage, rather than\n1866\tas evidence of generalization to unseen scenes. The frozen\n1867\testimator-ablation artifact contains predictions for 16,563 of\n1868\tthe 16,566 reconciled test-manifest observations; all ablation\n1869\tmetrics and visualizations below use this fixed scored subset.\n1870\tFor each visual observation, SoundSpaces 2.0 (Chen et al.\n1871\t2022) simulates a room impulse response using source and\n1872\tlistener positions in the same Matterport3D scene. We filter the impulse response in the octave band centered at 500\n1873\tHz, compute its energy decay curve by Schroeder integration (Schroeder 1965), and extrapolate the fitted decay slope\n1874\tto obtain the reference RT60 label. The extraction converts\n1875\tthe RIR to mono at its original sampling rate, applies a\n1876\tfifth-order Butterworth bandpass over 350–700 Hz, fits the\n1877\tSchroeder decay between −5 and −35 dB, and computes\n1878\tT60 = −60/slope. Across the final collection, the 500 Hz\n1879\tlabels have mean 0.519 s, standard deviation 0.342 s, median\n1880\t0.375 s, and range 0.082–2.230 s. The target is thus derived\n1881\tfrom the simulated acoustic response independently of the\n1882\tvisual inputs. These are simulated, derived labels rather than\n1883\tin-situ measured room-acoustic ground truth. This procedure produces 82,828 acoustically annotated RGB-D observations.\n1884\tFigure S1 shows representative observations and their\n1885\taligned visual-acoustic annotations.\n1886\t\n1887\tS3\n1888\t\n1889\tEvaluator Definitions and Validity\n1890\tCriteria\n1891\t\n1892\tAcoustiTrace first determines whether the evidence required\n1893\tby an evaluator is measurable and only then computes\n1894\tits physical-consistency score. Invalid outputs are excluded\n1895\tfrom the corresponding conditional mean rather than as-\n1896\t\n1897\t\fRGB observation\n1898\t\n1899\tDepth\n1900\t\n1901\tAcoustic Alpha Map\n1902\t\n1903\tRT60-500 label\n1904\tLarge hard interior\n1905\t1.3134 s (long)\n1906\tscene: gYvKGZ5eRqb / 186_270\n1907\t\n1908\tchurch; tall volume, stone/tile\n1909\tsurfaces\n1910\t\n1911\tSoft furnished room\n1912\t0.2495 s (very short)\n1913\tscene: XcA2TqTSSAj / 1_270\n1914\t\n1915\thome theater; recliners and\n1916\tcarpet\n1917\t\n1918\tLong corridor\n1919\t0.2359 s (very short)\n1920\tscene: 2n8kARJN3HM / 173_180\n1921\t\n1922\tgallery corridor; concrete\n1923\tfloor, exposed ceiling\n1924\t\n1925\tMixed-material open plan\n1926\t0.9007 s (medium)\n1927\tscene: X7HyMhZNoso / 142_0\n1928\t\n1929\tliving/dining/kitchen; glass,\n1930\ttile, furniture\n1931\t\n1932\tDepth is percentile-normalized per image for display.\n1933\t\n1934\tAlpha absorption:\n1935\t\n1936\tlow\n1937\t\n1938\tmid\n1939\t\n1940\thigh\n1941\t\n1942\tFigure S1: Representative acoustically annotated RGB-D observations. Each row shows an RGB observation, its aligned depth\n1943\tmap, the derived 500 Hz Acoustic Alpha Map, and the corresponding simulated 500 Hz RT60 label. The examples illustrate\n1944\tvariation in visible room geometry and spatially resolved surface absorption.\n1945\tsigned a score of zero. We distinguish three kinds of conditions throughout this section: programmatic checks implemented by the evaluator, constraints encouraged by prompt or\n1946\tdata construction, and physical assumptions that delimit interpretation but are not directly verified by code. All reported\n1947\tevaluator scores are mapped to [0, 100], with larger values\n1948\tindicating greater consistency for that dimension. These normalized scores are dimension-specific summaries rather than\n1949\ta shared physical unit and should not be compared as commensurate acoustic quantities across dimensions. Nevertheless, each evaluator is anchored by relation-consistent realworld samples in the high-score regime and validated through\n1950\tcontrolled perturbations that produce monotonic score degradation. Cross-dimension score patterns can therefore be interpreted descriptively as relative empirical difficulty within\n1951\tthe calibrated operating domains, but not as comparisons\n1952\tof absolute physical-error magnitude. The eight dimensions\n1953\tform a scoped set of measurable relations across the threestage acoustic process rather than an exhaustive inventory of\n1954\t\n1955\tphysical-acoustic phenomena. Future advances in detection\n1956\tand acoustic measurement can extend both their operating\n1957\tdomains and the relations covered by the benchmark.\n1958\t\n1959\tS3.1\n1960\t\n1961\tSound Generation\n1962\t\n1963\tMotion–Loudness Motion–Loudness requires localized\n1964\tvisible actions and corresponding audio events within the\n1965\tsame generated sample. The visual branch of OV-AVEL\n1966\t(Zhou et al. 2025) localizes candidate action intervals, while\n1967\tFlexSED (Hai et al. 2025) detects candidate sound events.\n1968\tTemporal association produces paired visual and audio segments. T2AV and I2AV use the same visual-ranking path: a\n1969\tQwen-based motion judgment with a frame-difference fallback ranks the localized actions using visual evidence only,\n1970\tconsidering motion amplitude, speed, range, and duration.\n1971\tFor each paired audio event, we compute the maximum\n1972\tshort-time RMS level in decibels within the matched window. The visual ordering and signed audio-level difference\n1973\treveal whether the stronger visible action is paired with the\n1974\t\n1975\t\flouder event. A sample is invalid when the required actions or\n1976\ttheir audio events cannot be localized or associated reliably.\n1977\tThe event association permits at most 0.35 s audio–visual\n1978\toffset and enforces at least 0.12 s between candidate events.\n1979\tAudio segments extend from 0.05 s before to 0.70 s after the\n1980\tmatched event; the frame-difference fallback samples video\n1981\tat 8 fps in a [−0.25, 0.10] s window around the visual peak.\n1982\tFor each valid pair, the no-margin indicator is one when the\n1983\tmaximum RMS level of the visually stronger action exceeds\n1984\tthat of the weaker action. The reported score is 100 times the\n1985\tmean of this indicator over valid pairs.\n1986\tLog Attack Time Log Attack Time is evaluated only for\n1987\tI2AV. The conditioning image and reference audio come\n1988\tfrom the same temporally aligned impact recording, providing the object and material context for the generated sample.\n1989\tThe evaluator localizes the generated and reference impacts\n1990\tand computes a smoothed RMS envelope for each matched\n1991\twindow. A pre-onset interval estimates the local baseline,\n1992\tand the transient peak defines the upper end of the envelope range. Attack duration is the interval between the first\n1993\tcrossings of 10% and 90% of this baseline-to-peak range;\n1994\ttaking its logarithm yields Log Attack Time. The evaluator measures the absolute difference between the generated\n1995\tand reference values. Audio is analyzed at 22,050 Hz using\n1996\t1,024-sample RMS frames, a 128-sample hop, and Gaussian\n1997\tsmoothing with σ = 1. The attack search window extends\n1998\tfrom 0.05 s before to 0.30 s after onset, with the preceding\n1999\t0.05 s used to estimate the baseline and attack times floored\n2000\tat 10−4 s. For generated and reference attack durations Tg\n2001\tand Tr , the normalized score is\n2002\t\u0012\n2003\t\u0013\n2004\t| log Tg − log Tr |\n2005\tSLAT = 100 exp −\n2006\t.\n2007\t(S3)\n2008\t0.35\n2009\tThe reference audio supplies the physical measurement and\n2010\tis not provided to the generator. A sample is invalid if either\n2011\timpact cannot be localized or its onset cannot be measured.\n2012\tImpact Decay Impact Decay applies to an isolated visible\n2013\timpact with a measurable post-impact audio segment. The\n2014\tevaluator extracts an audio window around the impact, computes a smoothed RMS envelope, locates the acoustic peak,\n2015\tand treats the post-peak portion as the decay interval. Audio\n2016\tis analyzed at 22,050 Hz in a segment from 0.05 s before\n2017\tto 0.70 s after the event. The exponential decay fit uses the\n2018\tinterval from 0.02 to 0.50 s after the acoustic peak and includes a residual energy floor. The segment must contain at\n2019\tleast 64 samples, at least five envelope points, and at least\n2020\tfive post-peak fit points. Let R2 be the decay-fit coefficient\n2021\tof determination, Etail the tail residual mean absolute error in decibels, and Dpf the peak-to-floor dynamic range in\n2022\tdecibels. The per-event decay-shape score is\n2023\t\u0012\n2024\t\u0013\n2025\tEtail\n2026\t2\n2027\tSdecay = 100 clip(R , 0, 1) exp −\n2028\t.\n2029\tmax(Dpf , 10−6 )\n2030\t(S4)\n2031\tA steadily decreasing tail with a small residual receives a\n2032\thigh score, whereas abrupt truncation, sustained plateaus,\n2033\tsecondary peaks, and irregular fluctuations reduce it. Validity\n2034\trequires a matched impact, sufficient usable decay support,\n2035\t\n2036\tand a finite decay-shape score. No minimum R2 threshold\n2037\tis imposed: R2 contributes continuously to the score rather\n2038\tthan serving as a separate hard validity gate. Overlapping\n2039\tevents, missing impacts, or segments without a usable decay\n2040\tinterval are outside the operating domain.\n2041\t\n2042\tS3.2\n2043\t\n2044\tPropagation Environment\n2045\t\n2046\tRT60 Consistency\n2047\tDesign rationale The visual estimator does not treat RT60\n2048\tas an unconstrained image regression target. A Sabineguided physics head captures the principal relation among\n2049\troom geometry, surface area, and absorption, while a regularized residual branch accounts for factors not visible from\n2050\ta single view. The residual branch is not intended to recover\n2051\tcomplete room-acoustic ground truth from unobserved scene\n2052\tcontent.\n2053\tVisual-side supervision The training target combines\n2054\tMatterport3D geometry, the PTB-derived Acoustic Alpha Map, and the 500 Hz RT60 label obtained from a\n2055\tSoundSpaces 2.0 RIR. The visual estimator uses Qwen3-VL8B-Instruct as its backbone. Each sample contains an RGB\n2056\timage, a depth map, an Acoustic Alpha Map, and a short\n2057\ttask prompt. The three images are processed using the native Qwen3-VL image representation. We take the last-layer\n2058\thidden states, apply attention-mask-aware mean pooling, and\n2059\tobtain a 4096-dimensional scene representation h.\n2060\tSabine-guided physics head The pooled representation is\n2061\tpassed through LayerNorm and dropout (p = 0.1), followed\n2062\tby separate geometry, absorption, residual, six-bin classification, and extreme-long heads. The geometry and absorption\n2063\tbranches predict positive latent quantities through softplus:\n2064\tVb = softplus(fV (h)) + ϵ,\n2065\t(S5)\n2066\tb500 = softplus(fA (h)) + ϵ,\n2067\tA\n2068\t\n2069\t(S6)\n2070\t!\n2071\t\n2072\tVb\n2073\tTbphys = clip 0.161\n2074\t, 0.05, 5.0 .\n2075\tb500\n2076\tA\n2077\t\n2078\t(S7)\n2079\t\n2080\tThe residual branch predicts a correction in log-RT60 space,\n2081\tgiving\n2082\t\u0010\n2083\t\u0010\n2084\t\u0011\n2085\t\u0011\n2086\tvisual\n2087\tTb60\n2088\t= clip exp log Tbphys + fres (h) , 0.05, 5.0 .\n2089\t(S8)\n2090\tb500 , the Sabine prior, the\n2091\tThe model therefore returns Vb , A\n2092\tresidual, the final 500 Hz RT60 estimate, six RT60-bin logits,\n2093\tand an extreme-long logit. The six bins are separated at 0.3,\n2094\t0.6, 1.0, 1.5, and 2.0 s.\n2095\tOptimization The Qwen3-VL base weights are frozen. We\n2096\ttrain LoRA adapters and the physics head; LoRA is applied to\n2097\tthe query, key, value, output, gate, up-projection, and downprojection modules with rank 16, scale 32, zero dropout,\n2098\tand no bias parameters. The objective combines log-domain\n2099\tSmoothL1 regression, six-bin cross entropy, extreme-long\n2100\tfocal binary cross entropy, physics consistency, residual L1\n2101\tregularization, and within-minibatch pairwise ranking:\n2102\tL = Lreg + 0.5Lclass + Llong\n2103\t(S9)\n2104\t+ 0.05Lphys + 0.01Lres + 0.1Lrank .\n2105\t\n2106\t\fEstimator\n2107\t\n2108\tMAE\n2109\t\n2110\tRMSE\n2111\t\n2112\tρ\n2113\t\n2114\tBias\n2115\t\n2116\tVanilla\n2117\tDirect SFT\n2118\tNumeric SFT\n2119\tSabine head\n2120\t\n2121\t1.2527\n2122\t0.0610\n2123\t0.0601\n2124\t0.0642\n2125\t\n2126\t1.3532\n2127\t0.1009\n2128\t0.0998\n2129\t0.0930\n2130\t\n2131\t0.2583\n2132\t0.8635\n2133\t0.8696\n2134\t0.8698\n2135\t\n2136\t1.2488\n2137\t-0.0223\n2138\t-0.0185\n2139\t-0.0010\n2140\t\n2141\tTable S3: Visual RT60 estimation on the held-out simulated test split. Rows denote vanilla Qwen3-VL, direct JSON\n2142\tLoRA SFT, numeric-weighted LoRA SFT, and the Sabineguided physics head. MAE, RMSE, and bias are in seconds;\n2143\tρ is Spearman correlation. Checkpoints are selected using\n2144\tthe validation split.\n2145\tReference RT60 interval\n2146\t0.0–0.3 s\n2147\t0.3–0.6 s\n2148\t0.6–1.0 s\n2149\t1.0–1.5 s\n2150\t> 1.5 s\n2151\t\n2152\tSabine-head MAE (s)\n2153\t0.0473\n2154\t0.0551\n2155\t0.0930\n2156\t0.0988\n2157\t0.1106\n2158\t\n2159\tTable S4: RT60-stratified error of the final visual estimator\n2160\ton the held-out simulated test split.\n2161\tThe regression tail weights for targets below 0.5 s, in\n2162\t[0.5, 1.0), [1.0, 1.5), [1.5, 2.0), and at least 2.0 s are 1, 2,\n2163\t4, 8, and 16, respectively. The class weights use the same\n2164\tincreasing-tail principle, with weights [1, 1, 2, 4, 8, 16]. The\n2165\textreme-long focal term uses positive weight 16 and focusing\n2166\tparameter 2; the ranking margin is 0.1 with at most 512 pairs\n2167\tper minibatch.\n2168\tTraining uses three epochs, per-device batch size 2, gradient accumulation over 16 steps, learning rate 2×10−4 , weight\n2169\tdecay 0.01, warmup ratio 0.03, cosine scheduling, bf16, gradient checkpointing, and maximum gradient norm 1. The\n2170\timage processor uses 12,544 and 401,408 as its minimum\n2171\tand maximum pixel budgets. Both the direct SFT baselines\n2172\tand the physics-head model select their best checkpoint using\n2173\tvalidation-set performance before evaluation on the held-out\n2174\tsimulated test split.\n2175\tDirect SFT baselines The direct baselines use the same\n2176\tQwen3-VL backbone and RGB, depth, and Acoustic Alpha Map inputs, but autoregressively produce a JSON field\n2177\tnamed rt60_500Hz rather than using Equation S8. Their\n2178\tbase weights are likewise frozen and the same LoRA modules are trained. Standard direct SFT uses token-level causal\n2179\tlanguage-model cross entropy. Numeric-weighted SFT assigns weight 5 to numeric target tokens and weight 1 to other\n2180\tsupervised tokens. The JSON baselines impose no modelside hard clamp; an unparseable numeric field is treated as a\n2181\tparse failure.\n2182\tTable S3 shows that direct SFT reduces MAE from 1.2527\n2183\tto 0.0610, and numeric-weighted SFT attains the lowest\n2184\tMAE of 0.0601. The Sabine-guided parameterization has an\n2185\tMAE of 0.0642 while achieving the lowest RMSE (0.0930),\n2186\tnear-zero bias (−0.0010), and a Spearman correlation of\n2187\t0.8698. Its error is higher in the shortest RT60 interval but\n2188\t\n2189\tlower than numeric-weighted SFT above 1.5 s (0.1106 versus\n2190\t0.1361 s), consistent with the physics head’s emphasis on the\n2191\tlong-reverberation tail. The scored test subset contains only\n2192\tnine examples with RT60 at least 2.0 s. The extreme-long\n2193\thead predicts six such examples, with recall 1/9 and precision 1/6; these values are reported as a tail diagnostic rather\n2194\tthan evidence of robust extreme-range performance.\n2195\tOperating range and limitations The learned output is\n2196\tnumerically bounded to [0.05, 5.0] s, whereas the simulated\n2197\tlabels used for training and evaluation span approximately\n2198\t0.08–2.23 s and are concentrated below 1.5 s. The quantitative evidence therefore supports aggregate performance\n2199\tover the simulated label range, with limited evidence in\n2200\tthe extreme-long tail. A single RGB-D observation cannot fully determine out-of-view geometry, room openings,\n2201\tceiling height, occluded furnishings, or hidden materials.\n2202\tMoreover, the Acoustic Alpha Map is a semantic-material\n2203\tabsorption proxy rather than an in-situ measurement. Real\n2204\trecordings and generated audio additionally introduce microphone, source, rendering, post-processing, and dynamicocclusion effects that are absent from the simulated labels.\n2205\tThe visual estimator is used within this defined operating domain and is not presented as a measurement-grade estimator\n2206\tfor arbitrary real rooms.\n2207\tThe benchmark validity decision requires a present,\n2208\tparseable visual prediction in the supported numeric range;\n2209\tit is not an explicit open-set or out-of-distribution detector.\n2210\tAudio-side apparent RT60 The audio estimator identifies\n2211\ta usable decay interval and computes a noise-compensated\n2212\tSchroeder energy decay curve after filtering the matched\n2213\twaveform in the octave band centered at 500 Hz. It returns\n2214\tan apparent RT60 only when the decay tail can be measured\n2215\tand fit. Repeated event sequences, no valid event candidate,\n2216\tinsufficient post-event tail, or insufficient dynamic range can\n2217\ttherefore invalidate the audio proxy. A matched\n2218\tis\n2219\t√ waveform\n2220\t√\n2221\tresampled to 24 kHz and filtered over [500/ 2, 500 2] Hz.\n2222\tCandidate impulses require at least 10 dB peak prominence;\n2223\tthe response starts 5 ms before the peak, and the noise floor is\n2224\testimated from 0.50 to 0.05 s before it. Noise-cutoff margins\n2225\tof 10 dB, with a 6 dB fallback, are evaluated using 10 ms\n2226\tframes and a 2 ms hop. A usable decay must span at least\n2227\t0.050 s, each candidate fit at least 0.020 s, and the candidate\n2228\tfit must attain R2 ≥ 0.65. T20, T10, and EDT candidates\n2229\tare considered in that order, and finite audio proxies outside [0.05, 5.0] s are rejected. A missing visual prediction or\n2230\tan incompatible audio–visual room class can also invalidate\n2231\tthe final comparison. Apparent RT60 estimated from generated audio is conceptually distinct from room RT60 measured from a controlled RIR. When both estimates are available, their discrepancy is measured by the absolute log ratio\n2232\taudio bvisual\n2233\tlog(Tb60\n2234\t/T60 ) . Visual predictions outside [0.08, 3.0]\n2235\taudio bvisual\n2236\ts are rejected. Writing r = Tb60\n2237\t/T60 , the consistency\n2238\tscore is\n2239\t\u0012\n2240\t\u0013\n2241\tlog 3 − | log r|\n2242\tSRT60 = 100 clip\n2243\t, 0, 1 .\n2244\t(S10)\n2245\tlog 3 − log 1.5\n2246\t\n2247\t\fThus ratios within a factor of 1.5 receive the maximum score\n2248\tand ratios at or beyond a factor of 3 receive zero.\n2249\tCausality Violation Causality Violation requires a localized visible contact and a matched audio event. The detected\n2250\taudio onset is compared with the visible contact time. Under the canonical scoring convention, an onset earlier than\n2251\tthe visible event by more than 1 ms is counted as a causality violation. Audio and visual detections are clustered with\n2252\tgaps of 0.20 s and 0.30 s, respectively, after filtering detections below confidence 0.20. Normal-mode matching allows\n2253\tup to 0.08 s early, 0.25 s synchronous, and 0.70 s delayed\n2254\tevidence; these tolerances define event association and are\n2255\tdistinct from the 1 ms scoring threshold. The 1 ms margin\n2256\tis the minimum timestamp increment used to implement a\n2257\tstrict temporal-precedence test; it does not imply 1 ms eventlocalization accuracy. For finite delays δi = taudio\n2258\t− tvisual\n2259\ti\n2260\ti\n2261\tin valid matched events, the conditional score is\n2262\t!\n2263\tn\n2264\t1X\n2265\tScausal = 100 1 −\n2266\t⊮[δi < −0.001] .\n2267\t(S11)\n2268\tn i=1\n2269\tSamples without a reliable matched event pair are excluded\n2270\tfrom this conditional score but remain in the denominator of\n2271\tthe separately reported output-level validity rate.\n2272\t\n2273\tS3.3\n2274\t\n2275\tAcoustic Reception\n2276\t\n2277\tRange Attenuation Range Attenuation evaluates relative\n2278\trange–level consistency within a screened operating domain.\n2279\tIn an ideal free field, the expected relative level change is\n2280\t∆L⋆ (t) = −20 log10\n2281\t\n2282\td(t)\n2283\t,\n2284\td(t0 )\n2285\t\n2286\t(S12)\n2287\t\n2288\twhere d(t) is the relative source range. The visual branch\n2289\ttracks the dominant visible source with Grounded-SAM, exports VDA-derived depth in point-cloud form, and aggregates source-mask-supported points over time to obtain a\n2290\trelative range trajectory. This trajectory is aligned with the\n2291\tshort-time audio level curve. Because source power can vary\n2292\tover a full clip, the comparison is performed in local sliding\n2293\twindows. Within each window, the observed relative level is\n2294\tcompared with Equation S12, and R2 measures how well the\n2295\treference explains the generated level trajectory. Programmatic screening checks track availability, depth validity, and\n2296\tapplicability of the range change. The implementation uses\n2297\t0.40 s windows with a 0.05 s stride. Visual depth is mediansmoothed over 0.35 s; a track requires at least six finite depth\n2298\tpoints, at least 50% finite support, and a reliable run of at least\n2299\t0.50 s, with gaps longer than 0.35 s splitting a run. Range\n2300\tscoring requires at least four finite trajectory points overall\n2301\tand at least three per scoring window. A dominant receding\n2302\tsegment must last at least 1.50 s and span at least 2.00 in the\n2303\tevaluator’s internal depth coordinate. These implementation\n2304\tthresholds do not imply metric distances. The evaluator does\n2305\tnot recover metric source–receiver distance or assume that\n2306\tarbitrary generated scenes satisfy ideal free-field conditions.\n2307\tApproximately stable source power, source directionality,\n2308\tand environmental reflections remain modeling assumptions\n2309\trather than directly verified validity conditions.\n2310\t\n2311\tApproach Gain Approach Gain uses intervals in which\n2312\tthe tracked source approaches the listener and the relative visual range decreases sufficiently to be measured. It evaluates\n2313\tthe monotonic agreement between the source-range trajectory and the short-time audio-level trajectory, together with\n2314\tthe orientation of the fitted loudness trend. Track, depth, and\n2315\tapplicability screening precede scoring; flattened, reversed,\n2316\tor temporally inconsistent gain patterns receive lower scores.\n2317\tIt uses the same 0.40 s windows and 0.05 s stride as Range\n2318\tAttenuation. A candidate window must span at least 0.15\n2319\tin the evaluator’s relative-depth coordinate and have fitted\n2320\tdepth slope below −0.002; the dominant approaching segment must last at least 1.50 s.\n2321\tLateral Stability Lateral Stability applies when the\n2322\ttracked source remains at approximately constant range or\n2323\tmoves primarily across the listener’s field of view. After selecting intervals with limited relative-depth variation, it measures systematic level drift and short-time loudness variation.\n2324\tThe score should be interpreted within this screened domain,\n2325\tnot as evidence that source power or the acoustic environment\n2326\tis globally stationary. The same sliding-window configuration is used, and candidate windows require relative-depth\n2327\tcoefficient of variation at most 0.12. Visual applicability\n2328\tadditionally requires image-plane bounding-box center displacement of at least 0.03 and path length of at least 0.05.\n2329\t\n2330\tS4\n2331\t\n2332\tPrompt Suite Construction\n2333\t\n2334\tThe prompt suites contain 605 unique T2AV prompts and\n2335\t748 unique I2AV prompts. Pilot prompt development used\n2336\tLTX-2.3 and, for its supported T2AV setting, JavisDiT++.\n2337\tThe remaining model families entered the evaluation only\n2338\tafter the prompt suites and validity criteria were frozen. Pilot refinement was limited to improving the observability\n2339\tand measurability of the target acoustic relation rather than\n2340\tselecting prompts according to a generator’s physical score.\n2341\tThe same final prompts and criteria were applied to all supported models, including the pilot models. The final reported\n2342\toutputs for LTX-2.3 and JavisDiT++ were regenerated after\n2343\tthe freeze.\n2344\tModel-independent validity criteria mean that the required visual and audio evidence, applicability thresholds,\n2345\textraction-success gates, and exclusion rules do not depend\n2346\ton model identity. Different models can therefore have different valid rates, but no model receives customized validity\n2347\tthresholds or prompt-specific exceptions.\n2348\tThe evaluator assignment counts are 84 for Range Attenuation, 222 each for Approach Gain and Lateral Stability, 110\n2349\tfor Motion–Loudness, 111 for Impact Decay, 138 for Causality Violation, and 84 for RT60 Consistency in T2AV. I2AV\n2350\tuses the same assignment counts and adds 143 Log Attack\n2351\tTime assignments. These are overlapping evaluator assignments rather than a partition of the unique prompt suites:\n2352\tApproach Gain and Lateral Stability share a receiver-motion\n2353\tpool, while Causality Violation reuses eligible prompts from\n2354\tthe Motion–Loudness and Impact Decay pools. Six prompts\n2355\tare also shared between the Range Attenuation pool and the\n2356\tcommon Approach Gain/Lateral Stability pool. Accordingly,\n2357\tthe unique T2AV count is 84 + 222 + 110 + 111 + 84 − 6 =\n2358\t\n2359\t\f605; adding the 143 I2AV-only Log Attack Time prompts\n2360\tgives 748 unique I2AV prompts.\n2361\tPrompt construction targets the operating domain of each\n2362\tevaluator: for example, isolated impacts for decay analysis,\n2363\tvisible near–far motion for Range Attenuation, and approximately constant-range motion for Lateral Stability. Prompts\n2364\tspecify the visual configuration needed to measure a relation. For Motion–Loudness, the two-action design balances\n2365\tphysical comparability against the limited temporal controllability of current generators; requiring additional actions\n2366\tat specified times would make prompt realization and event\n2367\tcorrespondence less reliable.\n2368\t\n2369\tS5\n2370\tS5.1\n2371\t\n2372\tFor source-action dimensions, prompts with missing contacts, unattributable body motion, soft or quasi-static interactions, continuous friction, or overlapping/repeated events\n2373\tare excluded. Impact Decay additionally requires an isolated\n2374\thard contact and an uninterrupted post-impact interval. RT60\n2375\tcandidates require an indoor overview, a static view, one short\n2376\tsource event, and sufficient event-free tail; views with cuts,\n2377\tcamera motion, speech, music, or competing activity are removed. I2AV Log Attack Time records must have a visible\n2378\tcontact object, a parseable paired reference recording, and a\n2379\tusable conditioning first frame.\n2380\t\n2381\tDeduplication and manifest checks Exact and\n2382\tnormalized-text duplicates are removed within each\n2383\tcandidate pool before dimension-stratified subsampling.\n2384\tSemantically similar impact candidates are additionally\n2385\tgrouped by environment, subject, object, interaction, and\n2386\tmaterial so that a narrow interaction type cannot dominate\n2387\tthe retained pool. Intentionally retained seed or index\n2388\tvariants are handled during stratified sampling rather than\n2389\tcounted as independent semantic coverage. Final manifest\n2390\tchecks verify required fields, file availability, task/evaluator\n2391\tmembership, and the assignment overlaps described above.\n2392\tThe manifest is then frozen before final generation; no\n2393\tprompt is added, removed, or rewritten according to a\n2394\tmodel’s physical-consistency score.\n2395\t\n2396\tModel Configurations\n2397\t\n2398\tWe evaluate LTX-2.3, JavisDiT++, NAVA, Ovi, UniVerse1, MOVA, Veo 3.1, Seedance 2.0, and Wan 2.7 using each\n2399\tmodel’s supported generation settings. Log Attack Time is\n2400\tI2AV-only because the conditioning image identifies the\n2401\tstruck object and material context. The evaluated JavisDiT++ release does not provide a native I2AV mode, while\n2402\tUniVerse-1 does not provide a native text-only T2AV mode;\n2403\tthese task combinations are therefore marked unavailable.\n2404\t\n2405\tS5.2\n2406\tScreening and quality control Candidate prompts are\n2407\tassembled through dimension-specific pipelines: receivermotion candidates for Range Attenuation, Approach Gain,\n2408\tand Lateral Stability; source-action candidates for Motion–\n2409\tLoudness; isolated-contact candidates for Impact Decay and\n2410\tCausality Violation; indoor RGB-D views for RT60 Consistency; and paired first-frame/reference records for Log Attack Time. Candidate rewriting retains only the visual and\n2411\tevent structure needed to make the target relation observable.\n2412\tReceiver candidates are screened for an external fixed viewpoint, one dominant trackable source, a stable background,\n2413\tand sufficient visible range or image-plane motion. Interior\n2414\tor attached-camera views, toy/miniature sources, competing\n2415\tsources, and trajectories without a measurable range pattern\n2416\tare removed.\n2417\t\n2418\tBenchmark Protocol and Additional\n2419\tResults\n2420\t\n2421\tValidity Rates and Exclusion Criteria\n2422\t\n2423\tTables S6 and S7 report the fraction of generated outputs\n2424\tthat pass the applicability and measurement checks for each\n2425\tevaluator. Scores in the main paper are conditional means\n2426\tover these valid outputs.\n2427\tAggregate invalid coverage Across the 120 supported\n2428\tmodel–task–evaluator cells (56 T2AV and 64 I2AV), the\n2429\tbenchmark contains 16,680 output–evaluator measurement\n2430\tattempts. Of these, 649 of 7,768 T2AV attempts (8.35%) and\n2431\t993 of 8,912 I2AV attempts (11.14%) are invalid.\n2432\tInvalid-reason audit We audit the available per-output\n2433\treason and status fields using a conservative taxonomy. Evidence tags can overlap, and a record is left as unknown when\n2434\tthe available fields establish invalidity but do not support a\n2435\tmore specific attribution. The audit is used to explain failure\n2436\tmechanisms; exact validity counts remain those in Tables S6\n2437\tand S7.\n2438\tRange Attenuation. Most invalid Range records lack a\n2439\tfinite primary score without exposing a more specific failure\n2440\treason and are therefore retained as unknown. A smaller set\n2441\texplicitly indicates missing audio or an unavailable visual\n2442\ttrack/depth input.\n2443\tApproach Gain. Invalid outputs primarily fail the visual\n2444\tapplicability gate because an approaching interval, depth trajectory, or reliable track cannot be verified. Missing or nearsilent audio and extraction failures account for additional\n2445\tI2AV cases.\n2446\tLateral Stability. All T2AV outputs pass the Lateral Stability gate. The I2AV invalid cases mainly reflect unverifiable constant-range/lateral applicability or missing audio,\n2447\twith ambiguous records retained as unknown.\n2448\tMotion–Loudness. Both tasks use the same Qwen-based\n2449\tmotion judgment and frame-difference fallback. Invalidity\n2450\tarises when this path cannot establish a reliable visual ordering, when too few actions or audio events can be localized\n2451\tand matched, or when the generation or evaluator input is\n2452\tincomplete.\n2453\tImpact Decay. The main failure modes are a missing or\n2454\tunmatched impact, insufficient post-impact support for a finite decay fit, and failed or incomplete evaluator input. As\n2455\tdescribed above, a successful finite fit is required but R2 is\n2456\tnot used as an additional hard threshold.\n2457\tCausality Violation. Outputs are invalid when the evaluator finds too few matched audio–visual events or clusters,\n2458\t\n2459\t\fDimension\n2460\t\n2461\tRepresentative prompt excerpt\n2462\t\n2463\tEvidence controlled by the\n2464\tprompt\n2465\t\n2466\tRange Attenuation\n2467\t\n2468\tA fixed street-side view with one car moving steadily from the foreground\n2469\tinto the distance against a stable background.\n2470\tA fixed roadside view with one race car moving from a distant position toward\n2471\tthe camera.\n2472\t\n2473\tSingle trackable source and\n2474\tsustained receding trajectory.\n2475\tSingle trackable source and\n2476\tsustained approaching trajectory.\n2477\tImage-plane motion with limited relative-depth variation.\n2478\tTwo separated contacts differing primarily in visible\n2479\tstrength.\n2480\tIsolated hard transient and\n2481\tmeasurable post-impact tail.\n2482\tLocalizable visual contact\n2483\ttimes and attributable sound\n2484\tevents.\n2485\tMaterial/contact context and a\n2486\tmeasurable reference attack.\n2487\tVisible\n2488\troom\n2489\tgeometry/materials and an isolated\n2490\treverberant tail.\n2491\t\n2492\tApproach Gain\n2493\t\n2494\tLateral Stability\n2495\tMotion–Loudness\n2496\t\n2497\tA fixed exterior view with one source crossing the scene at approximately\n2498\tconstant apparent range and size.\n2499\tA fixed workshop view in which the same person uses the same hammer on\n2500\tthe same wooden object, first lightly and then more forcefully.\n2501\t\n2502\tImpact Decay\n2503\t\n2504\tA fixed workshop view with one visible hammer–chisel impact on a wooden\n2505\tboard followed by a still interval and no second strike.\n2506\tCausality Violation A fixed court view with clearly visible, temporally separated racket–ball\n2507\tcontacts.\n2508\tLog Attack Time\n2509\tRT60 Consistency\n2510\t\n2511\tAn I2AV first frame showing a close-up metal locker and a paired reference\n2512\trecording containing one visible stick impact.\n2513\tA static wide indoor-room view with one short hand clap followed by several\n2514\tseconds of stillness.\n2515\t\n2516\tRepresentative prompt excerpts for the eight dimensions. RT60 Consistency follows the final 84-prompt assignment.\n2517\tModel/configuration\n2518\t\n2519\tMode\n2520\t\n2521\tVideo output\n2522\t\n2523\tSteps\n2524\t\n2525\tKey generation settings\n2526\t\n2527\tLTX-2.3 distilled 1.1\n2528\tLTX-2.3 distilled 1.1\n2529\tJavisDiT-v1.0-jav\n2530\tNAVA FP8\n2531\tNAVA FP8\n2532\tOvi\n2533\tUniVerse-1\n2534\tMOVA\n2535\t\n2536\tT2AV\n2537\tI2AV\n2538\tT2AV\n2539\tT2AV\n2540\tI2AV\n2541\tT2AV, I2AV\n2542\tI2AV\n2543\tT2AV, I2AV\n2544\t\n2545\t512×768, 121 frames at 24 fps Default Distilled-pipeline defaults\n2546\t512×768, 121 frames at 24 fps Default First-frame conditioning, strength 1.0\n2547\t240p, 9:16, 65 frames at 16 fps\n2548\t50\n2549\tCFG = 5.0\n2550\t1280×704, ∼ 6 s at 24 fps\n2551\t50\n2552\t37-frame latent setting; fixed seed 123\n2553\t1280×704, ∼ 6 s at 24 fps\n2554\t50\n2555\t37-frame latent setting; video/audio guidance = 3.0/2.0\n2556\t720×720\n2557\t50\n2558\tUniPC, shift = 5.0, video/audio guidance = 4.0/3.0\n2559\t256×256, 125 frames\n2560\t35\n2561\tVideo/audio guidance = 1.2/5.0\n2562\t352×640, 121 frames at 24 fps\n2563\t50\n2564\tCFG = 5.0, sigma shift = 5.0\n2565\t\n2566\tTable S5: Verified output and sampling settings for locally executed model–task configurations.\n2567\tsubstantial unmatched event evidence, or no finite delay estimate.\n2568\tRT60 Consistency. The dominant evidence concerns an\n2569\tunavailable audio RT60 proxy, including repeated event sequences, no valid event candidate, insufficient post-event tail,\n2570\tor insufficient dynamic range. Missing visual predictions and\n2571\taudio–visual room-class mismatches can overlap with these\n2572\taudio-side failures.\n2573\t\n2574\tS5.3\n2575\t\n2576\tConditional Means and Bootstrap\n2577\tConfidence Intervals\n2578\t\n2579\tEach prompt yields exactly one generated output for a given\n2580\tmodel. Within each model–task–evaluator cell, we retain the\n2581\tvalid generated outputs and draw 10,000 bootstrap resamples of the same size with replacement. We recompute the\n2582\tconditional mean for every resample and report the 2.5th and\n2583\t97.5th percentiles as the 95% confidence interval. Resampling is performed independently within each cell using a\n2584\tfixed cell-specific random seed. We do not apply promptlevel clustering because there are no repeated outputs for the\n2585\t\n2586\tsame prompt within a model. Tables S8 and S9 report the\n2587\tresulting intervals.\n2588\t\n2589\tS5.4\n2590\t\n2591\tMatched-Valid Analysis\n2592\t\n2593\tConditional scoring can favor a low-coverage model if only\n2594\tits easiest outputs remain valid. A matched-valid analysis\n2595\taddresses this concern by retaining only prompts for which\n2596\tevery compared model yields a valid score. For each evaluator, we intersect the valid prompt sets of the compared models\n2597\tand recompute their conditional means on that shared subset. We analyze LTX-2.3, JavisDiT++, and Ovi for T2AV, and\n2598\tLTX-2.3, Ovi, and MOVA for I2AV. These groups include a\n2599\thigh-coverage reference and models with lower coverage on\n2600\tdifferent evaluators. The resulting percentile 95% confidence\n2601\tintervals use 10,000 prompt-level bootstrap resamples.\n2602\tWithin this three-model comparison, the ordering is unchanged from Table S8 for Range Attenuation, Approach\n2603\tGain, Lateral Stability, Motion–Loudness, Impact Decay, and\n2604\tCausality Violation. RT60 Consistency is the only exception:\n2605\tJavisDiT++ and Ovi exchange their closely matched order,\n2606\t\n2607\t\fModel\n2608\t\n2609\tRange Approach\n2610\t84/84\n2611\t(100.00)\n2612\t82/84\n2613\t(97.62)\n2614\t84/84\n2615\t(100.00)\n2616\t65/84\n2617\t(77.38)\n2618\t\n2619\t222/222\n2620\t(100.00)\n2621\t222/222\n2622\t(100.00)\n2623\t222/222\n2624\t(100.00)\n2625\t220/222\n2626\t(99.10)\n2627\t\n2628\t59/84\n2629\t(70.24)\n2630\t84/84\n2631\tVeo 3.1\n2632\t(100.00)\n2633\t84/84\n2634\tSeedance 2.0 (100.00)\n2635\t84/84\n2636\tWan 2.7\n2637\t(100.00)\n2638\t\n2639\t222/222\n2640\t(100.00)\n2641\t222/222\n2642\t(100.00)\n2643\t222/222\n2644\t(100.00)\n2645\t222/222\n2646\t(100.00)\n2647\t\n2648\tLTX-2.3\n2649\tJavisDiT++\n2650\tNAVA\n2651\tOvi\n2652\tUniVerse-1\n2653\tMOVA\n2654\t\n2655\tLateral\n2656\t\n2657\tMotion\n2658\t\n2659\tImpact Causality\n2660\t\n2661\