from the same\n3309\trecording are not treated as independent samples.\n3310\tThe endpoint changes in the severity sweeps plotted in\n3311\tFigure 6(a) of the main paper provide a direct summary for\n3312\tfive dimensions. Relative to the unmodified condition, 12\n3313\tdB attenuation of the stronger-action event reduces the mean\n3314\tMotion–Loudness score by 65.0 points. The strongest decaytail perturbation reduces Impact Decay by 33.0 points, complete range-envelope flattening reduces Range Attenuation\n3315\tby 21.8 points and Approach Gain by 13.1 points, and 8 dB\n3316\tinjected fluctuation reduces Lateral Stability by 34.1 points.\n3317\tThe final clean-anchor Causality Violation sweep retains 40\n3318\t\n3319\tsources that are common-valid across the tested onset advances; its mean score decreases as the audio is advanced\n3320\tfrom 0 to 120 ms.\n3321\tLog Attack Time dose response The strict final analysis\n3322\tstarts from 44 high-score identity anchors and retains the 17\n3323\tsources that remain valid at attack-duration factors 1, 1.5,\n3324\t2, and 4. On this common-valid subset, the median withinsource Spearman correlation between stretch factor and measured Log Attack Time is 0.7746, with a source-bootstrap\n3325\t95% confidence interval of [0.6325, 0.9487]. The corresponding correlation between perturbation severity and consistency score is −0.7746 [-0.9487, -0.4000]. A 4× stretch\n3326\treduces the score by 34.68 points on average [25.57, 43.33],\n3327\twith degradation in 82.4% of retained anchors (one-sided\n3328\tWilcoxon p = 6.1 × 10−5 ).\n3329\tThe response is deliberately interpreted at the evaluator’s\n3330\ttemporal resolution rather than as a continuously strict curve.\n3331\tIts envelope hop is 5.805 ms, and 35 of the 44 identity anchors\n3332\toccupy the same two-hop attack-time bin. Consequently, 31\n3333\t\n3334\t\f(a) Visual RT60 estimator\n3335\ton the simulated test split\n3336\t\n3337\t102\n3338\t\n3339\t1.50\n3340\t1.25\n3341\t1.00\n3342\t\n3343\t101\n3344\t\n3345\t0.75\n3346\t0.50\n3347\t0.25\n3348\t\n3349\tBinned median\n3350\t\n3351\tVisually predicted RT60-500 proxy (s)\n3352\t\n3353\tPredicted RT60-500 (s)\n3354\t\n3355\t1.75\n3356\t\n3357\t0.7\n3358\t\n3359\tn = 16,563\n3360\tMAE = 0.0642 s\n3361\tRMSE = 0.0930 s\n3362\tρ = 0.8698\n3363\tlog10 count\n3364\t\n3365\t2.00\n3366\t\n3367\t100\n3368\t\n3369\t0.5\n3370\t\n3371\t1.0\n3372\t\n3373\t1.5\n3374\t\n3375\t2.0\n3376\t\n3377\t0.6\n3378\t\n3379\t(b) Audio-visual proxy agreement\n3380\ton STARSS23\n3381\tn = 26\n3382\tMAE = 0.0791 s\n3383\tMedian ratio error = 1.206x\n3384\t\n3385\t0.5\n3386\t\n3387\t0.4\n3388\t\n3389\t0.3\n3390\t\n3391\t0.2\n3392\t0.2\n3393\t\n3394\tReference RT60-500 (s)\n3395\t\n3396\t0.3\n3397\t\n3398\t0.4\n3399\t\n3400\t0.5\n3401\t\n3402\t0.6\n3403\t\n3404\t0.7\n3405\t\n3406\tAudio-derived apparent RT60-500 (s)\n3407\t\n3408\tFigure S2: Visual RT60 validation. (a) Predictions from the Sabine-guided physics head on the 16,563 scored observations in\n3409\tthe held-out simulated test subset. Hexagons show sample density, the orange curve shows binned medians, and the dashed line\n3410\tdenotes identity. (b) Agreement between independently audio-derived apparent RT60 and visually predicted RT60 proxies for\n3411\tthe 26 valid STARSS23 clapping windows. STARSS23 supplies event annotations rather than room-level RT60 ground truth;\n3412\tboth axes in panel (b) are proxies.\n3413\tof the 51 adjacent common-valid comparisons are quantization ties, while only 2 of 51 are true reversals. This resolution\n3414\taudit explains the staircase response in Figure 6(a) without\n3415\ttreating unchanged adjacent bins as independent failures.\n3416\tRT60 mismatch dose response For controlled RT60 validation, we fix the visual inputs and visual RT60 proxy for\n3417\t26 anchors and replace only the audio with a fixed dry\n3418\timpact convolved with synthetic 500 Hz room impulse responses. Six target audio-to-visual RT60 ratios, 0.50, 0.67,\n3419\t1.00, 1.50, 2.00, and 3.00, produce 156 valid conditions with\n3420\tno skipped estimates. In log-ratio space, the measured ratio\n3421\ttracks the injected target with slope 0.9808, intercept 0.0544,\n3422\tR2 = 0.8743, and mean absolute log residual 0.1750.\n3423\tMore importantly, the median within-anchor Spearman\n3424\tcorrelation between injected mismatch severity and consistency score is −0.7827, with a source-bootstrap 95% confidence interval of [-0.8874, -0.7392]. At 2× and 3× mismatch, all 26 anchors decrease in score, with median drops\n3425\tof 44.22 and 100.00 points, respectively. Ratios near 0.67,\n3426\t1.00, and 1.50 frequently saturate by design because Equation S10 assigns the maximum score within the predefined\n3427\t1.5× tolerance region.\n3428\tTogether, relation recovery and controlled perturbation\n3429\ttest validity in complementary directions: the former asks\n3430\twhether an expected acoustic relation can be recovered when\n3431\tit is present and measurable, whereas the latter asks whether\n3432\ta targeted violation is detected when the visual content and\n3433\tnon-target semantics are held fixed.\n3434\t\n3435\tS6.3\n3436\t\n3437\tProxy Agreement on STARSS23\n3438\t\n3439\tWe further evaluate RT60 Consistency on real indoor recordings from STARSS23, the Sony-TAu Realistic Spatial Soundscapes 2023 dataset (Sony and Tampere University 2023;\n3440\tDCASE Challenge 2023). STARSS23 provides synchronized\n3441\tmultichannel audio and 360-degree video from real rooms,\n3442\ttogether with temporal sound-event labels, directions of arrival, and source distances. Its development data cover 16\n3443\trooms and 13 target event classes, including Clapping. These\n3444\tannotations identify candidate clapping intervals; they do not\n3445\tprovide RT60 labels and are not treated as room-acoustic\n3446\tground truth in this experiment.\n3447\tWe extract 44 candidate clapping windows using the\n3448\tdataset-provided event class and temporal annotations. We\n3449\tthen apply the same 500 Hz audio-side decay analysis used by\n3450\tthe benchmark to derive an apparent RT60 from the recorded\n3451\twaveform. Windows without an isolated, measurable decay\n3452\tare rejected, primarily because of strong background noise or\n3453\tinsufficient energy in the 500 Hz band. This leaves 26 valid\n3454\twindows. For each valid window, the visual RT60 estimator\n3455\tpredicts a 500 Hz RT60 proxy from the temporally aligned\n3456\tvideo observation. The analysis therefore compares an independently derived audio proxy with a model-predicted visual\n3457\tproxy.\n3458\tWriting Ta and Tv for the audio and visual proxies, we\n3459\t\n3460\t\fMotion–Loudness evidence\n3461\t\n3462\tMotion–Loudness\n3463\t\n3464\tM1\n3465\t\n3466\tM1\n3467\tdB\n3468\t\n3469\te1: -29.2 dB\n3470\tact 1 swing @ 0.29s\n3471\t\n3472\tact 1 contact @ 0.45s\n3473\t\n3474\tact 1 follow @ 0.61s\n3475\t\n3476\te2: -39.5 dB\n3477\t\n3478\tact 2 swing @ 2.13s\n3479\t\n3480\tact 2 contact @ 2.45s\n3481\t\n3482\tact 2 follow @ 2.61s\n3483\t\n3484\texpected louder: event 2; measured delta = -10.3 dB\n3485\tscore = 0.0 (pairwise order violated)\n3486\t\n3487\tLog Attack Time evidence\n3488\t\n3489\tLog Attack Time\n3490\t\n3491\tAT1\n3492\t\n3493\tAT1\n3494\tnorm. env.\n3495\t\n3496\tref -0.05s\n3497\t\n3498\tref onset @ 0.34s\n3499\t\n3500\tref +0.16s\n3501\t\n3502\tgen onset @ 0.01s\n3503\t\n3504\tgen +0.16s\n3505\t\n3506\tgen +0.32s\n3507\t\n3508\treference\n3509\t\n3510\tgenerated\n3511\t\n3512\tLAT error = 0.139 s; ratio error = 12.000\n3513\tscore = 4.1\n3514\t\n3515\tImpact Decay evidence\n3516\t\n3517\tImpact Decay\n3518\t\n3519\tI1\n3520\t\n3521\tI1\n3522\trel. dB\n3523\t\n3524\tt-0.30 @ 0.15s\n3525\t\n3526\tt-0.15 @ 0.30s\n3527\t\n3528\timpact @ 0.45s\n3529\t\n3530\tt+0.15 @ 0.60s\n3531\t\n3532\ttail @ 0.80s\n3533\t\n3534\ttail @ 1.05s\n3535\t\n3536\tenvelope\n3537\t\n3538\tfit\n3539\t\n3540\tdecay R^2 = 0.928; max drop = 1.38 dB\n3541\tscore = 92.8 (decay-fit)\n3542\t\n3543\tRT60 Consistency evidence\n3544\t\n3545\tRT60 Consistency\n3546\t\n3547\tRT1\n3548\t\n3549\tRT1\n3550\t\n3551\taudio\n3552\t0.232s\n3553\t\n3554\tvisual\n3555\t0.374s\n3556\t\n3557\tRGB visual input\n3558\t\n3559\tdepth map (display-normalized)\n3560\t\n3561\tabs error = 0.141 s; ratio error = 1.608x\n3562\tscore = 89.9\n3563\t\n3564\tFigure S3: Representative diagnostic readouts for Motion–Loudness, Log Attack Time, Impact Decay, and RT60 Consistency.\n3565\tEach row pairs the evaluator’s required evidence with the corresponding native acoustic measurement and final score.\n3566\treport\n3567\teabs = |Ta − Tv |,\n3568\t(S13)\n3569\telog = | log Ta − log Tv |,\n3570\t(S14)\n3571\teratio = max(Ta /Tv , Tv /Ta ).\n3572\t(S15)\n3573\tWe also apply the benchmark consistency mapping in Equation S10. Table S12 summarizes the resulting proxy agreement.\n3574\tOf the 26 valid pairs, 10 have absolute error at most 0.05\n3575\ts, 17 at most 0.10 s, and 23 at most 0.15 s. Thirteen pairs\n3576\t\n3577\tare within a factor of 1.20×, 18 are within 1.25×, and all 26\n3578\tare within 1.75×. The median ratio error of approximately\n3579\t1.21×, together with a mean absolute error of 0.079 s, summarizes agreement between the two proxies on this screened\n3580\treal-recording subset.\n3581\t\n3582\tS6.4\n3583\t\n3584\tMeasured-RIR Case Study on BRAS CR3\n3585\t\n3586\tWe additionally examine one measured-room case from\n3587\tthe Benchmark for Room Acoustical Simulation (BRAS)\n3588\t\n3589\t\fStatistic\n3590\tCandidate clapping windows\n3591\tValid paired proxies\n3592\tMean absolute error\n3593\tMedian absolute error\n3594\tMean absolute log error\n3595\tMedian ratio error\n3596\tMean consistency score\n3597\tMedian consistency score\n3598\tMinimum consistency score\n3599\t\n3600\tValue\n3601\t44\n3602\t26 (59.1%)\n3603\t0.0791 s\n3604\t0.0739 s\n3605\t0.2013\n3606\t1.2063×\n3607\t98.56\n3608\t100.00\n3609\t85.51\n3610\t\n3611\t(a) RGB observation\n3612\t\n3613\t(b) Estimated depth\n3614\t\n3615\t(c) Image-derived Acoustic\n3616\tAlpha Map\n3617\t\n3618\t(d) 500 Hz comparison\n3619\tVisual prediction\n3620\t1.1019 s\n3621\t\n3622\tTable S12: Agreement between audio-derived and visually\n3623\tpredicted 500 Hz RT60 proxies on the valid STARSS23 clapping subset.\n3624\t\n3625\tBRAS room-level T20\n3626\t1.2584 s\n3627\t\n3628\t(Brinkmann et al. 2021; Aspöck et al. 2020). BRAS was\n3629\tcreated to support reproducible room-acoustics comparisons\n3630\tand provides documented room geometry, surfaces, source–\n3631\treceiver configurations, and measured acoustic parameters.\n3632\tWe select its CR3 complex music-room scene because it\n3633\tcomplements the event-centered STARSS23 analysis: CR3\n3634\tprovides a measured RIR-derived reverberation comparator,\n3635\twhile its room volume, stage, wood floor, hard boundaries,\n3636\tand seating supply diverse visual geometry and absorption\n3637\tcues.\n3638\tCR3 was not used for RT60 training, hyperparameter tuning, or checkpoint selection. The visual pipeline receives an\n3639\tRGB view, a Video-Depth-Anything depth estimate, and a\n3640\tGrounded-SAM material segmentation mapped to a 500 Hz\n3641\tAcoustic Alpha Map using the same image-derived procedure as the benchmark; it does not use BRAS surface descriptions or measured acoustics as input. The visual estimator predicts 1.1019 s in the 500 Hz octave band. The official\n3642\tBRAS file reports a room-level 500 Hz T20 of 1.2584 s,\n3643\twithout identifying that value as a mean or median over individual RIRs. Their absolute difference is 0.1565 s (12.4%\n3644\trelative error; ratio error 1.142×). This case therefore shows\n3645\tthat the visual pipeline recovers a plausible measured-room\n3646\treverberation scale from independently inferred geometry\n3647\tand material cues.\n3648\tBRAS categorizes CR1–CR4 as complex scenes with\n3649\tgreater measurement uncertainty and cautions against treating them as direct reference scenes. Accordingly, we use\n3650\tCR3 as a single illustrative measured-RIR agreement case,\n3651\tnot as an aggregate external-validation estimate or evidence\n3652\tof generalization across BRAS rooms.\n3653\t\n3654\tS6.5\n3655\t\n3656\tHuman Evaluation Protocol\n3657\t\n3658\tControlled sensitivity alone does not establish that an evaluator’s preference matches human perception of the target relation. We therefore test human alignment using cross-model\n3659\toutputs matched by prompt, task, and dimension; real-world\n3660\tanchors and perturbed validation samples are excluded. Each\n3661\tdimension has eight prompts and eight unique pairs, one pair\n3662\tper prompt. Each of the 30 expert raters judges every pair exactly once, yielding 30 ratings per pair and 1,920 judgments\n3663\tin total. Model identities and evaluator scores are hidden,\n3664\t\n3665\t|error| = 0.1565 s (12.4%)\n3666\t0.05\n3667\t0.25\n3668\t0.45\n3669\tEstimated absorption coefficient α500\n3670\t\n3671\t0.00 0.25 0.50 0.75 1.00 1.25 1.50\n3672\t\n3673\tReverberation time at 500 Hz (s)\n3674\t\n3675\tFigure S4: Measured-RIR case study on BRAS CR3. The visual estimator receives an RGB observation, independently\n3676\testimated depth, and an image-derived 500 Hz Acoustic Alpha Map. CR3 was excluded from training, tuning, and checkpoint selection, and no BRAS surface descriptions or measured acoustics were used as model input. The prediction is\n3677\t1.1019 s, compared with the official room-level 500 Hz T20\n3678\tof 1.2584 s. This single-room result is illustrative rather than\n3679\tan aggregate BRAS evaluation.\n3680\tleft–right order is randomized, and raters judge only the target acoustic relation using A, B, or Tie. Table S13 reports\n3681\tagreement between evaluator preferences and human judgments.\n3682\t\n3683\tS6.6\n3684\t\n3685\tRepresentative Evaluator Readouts\n3686\t\n3687\tFigures S3 and S5 make the native diagnostic outputs concrete with one screened example per dimension. In each row,\n3688\tthe left panel shows the visual or paired-reference evidence\n3689\tused by the evaluator, and the right panel shows its event\n3690\tmarkers, fitted quantity, trajectory, or consistency comparison. Scores are reported on the benchmark’s 0–100 scale.\n3691\tThese examples illustrate the evaluator readouts and are not\n3692\tadditional aggregate estimates.\n3693\t\n3694\tS7\n3695\t\n3696\tComparison with PhyAVBench CPRS\n3697\t\n3698\tPhyAVBench evaluates several physical relations in generated audio–video and its official CPRS metric measures\n3699\twhether the embedding transition between a paired condition follows a learned reference direction (Xie et al.\n3700\t2025). We use its inverse-square-law T34 subset because\n3701\tdistance-dependent attenuation is the direct conceptual overlap with AcoustiTrace Range Attenuation. Holding the official prompts and near–far pairing fixed isolates the difference\n3702\tbetween an embedding-direction metric and a diagnostic that\n3703\tcompares the generated visual distance change with the accompanying audio-level change.\n3704\t\n3705\t\fCausality Violation evidence\n3706\t\n3707\tCausality Violation\n3708\t\n3709\tC1\n3710\t\n3711\tC1\n3712\tdB\n3713\taudio onset\n3714\tvisual contact\n3715\t\n3716\tpre audio @ 0.25s\n3717\t\n3718\taudio onset @ 0.40s\n3719\t\n3720\tbetween @ 0.43s\n3721\t\n3722\tvisual @ 0.45s\n3723\t\n3724\tvisual +0.15s\n3725\t\n3726\tvisual +0.30s\n3727\t\n3728\tdelay = audio onset - visual contact = -0.050 s\n3729\trule: delay < -0.001 s -> score = 0; otherwise score = 100\n3730\t\n3731\tscore = 0.0 (audio precedes contact)\n3732\t\n3733\tRange Attenuation evidence\n3734\t\n3735\tRange Attenuation\n3736\t\n3737\tR1\n3738\t\n3739\tR1\n3740\tnorm.\n3741\t\n3742\tnear/start @ 0.2s\n3743\t\n3744\tnear @ 1.0s\n3745\t\n3746\tmid-near @ 1.8s\n3747\t\n3748\tmid-far @ 2.6s\n3749\t\n3750\tfar @ 3.4s\n3751\t\n3752\tfar/end @ 4.2s\n3753\t\n3754\tvisual range proxy\n3755\t\n3756\tsound level\n3757\t\n3758\tR^2 = 0.001; rho = -0.318; slope = -0.090\n3759\t\n3760\tscore = 0.1\n3761\t\n3762\tApproach Gain evidence\n3763\t\n3764\tApproach Gain\n3765\t\n3766\tA1\n3767\t\n3768\tA1\n3769\tnorm.\n3770\t\n3771\tfar/start @ 0.2s\n3772\t\n3773\tfar @ 1.0s\n3774\t\n3775\tmid-far @ 1.8s\n3776\t\n3777\tmid-near @ 2.6s\n3778\t\n3779\tnear @ 3.4s\n3780\t\n3781\tnear/end @ 4.2s\n3782\t\n3783\tvisual range proxy\n3784\t\n3785\tsound level\n3786\t\n3787\tapproach score = 53.9; fit R^2 = 0.071\n3788\t\n3789\tscore = 53.9\n3790\t\n3791\tLateral Stability evidence\n3792\t\n3793\tLateral Stability\n3794\t\n3795\tS1\n3796\t\n3797\tS1\n3798\tnorm.\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "1b7c9db4-91e8-4726-8422-943fa4cb9d83", "uuid": "813d1002-4690-4710-b3c1-446e8a298d54", "timestamp": "2026-08-04T06:17:26.357Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/acoustrace.txt", "content": "Motion\n\nImpact\n\nCausality\n\nLAT\n\nRT60\n\n66.89\n91.33\n84.95\n88.39\n65.22\n72.73\n62.56\n42.12\nLTX-2.3\n[62.08–71.63] [62.76–67.57] [90.04–92.41] [63.82–81.63] [81.12–88.45] [82.61–93.46] [58.01–67.16] [34.67–49.67]\nJavisDiT++\nNot available\n86.91\n89.55\n65.29\n67.78\n65.88\n83.64\n87.80\n70.71\nNAVA\n[63.07–72.52] [63.08–68.71] [85.48–88.19] [82.71–95.57] [79.85–87.20] [82.00–92.87] [60.33–70.39] [64.08–77.17]\n71.98\n91.08\n66.91\n45.30\n54.33\n83.20\n50.60\n50.42\nOvi\n[39.55–51.11] [69.57–74.25] [90.03–92.00] [43.90–64.76] [80.23–86.15] [38.48–62.12] [44.06–56.80] [60.11–73.49]\n44.97\n32.53\n65.81\n84.70\n50.77\n63.13\n34.65\n42.06\nUniVerse-1\n[20.81–40.67] [63.47–68.22] [83.38–85.87] [37.50–63.27