{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b4261e8dbe37b5ad", "type": "tool_result", "content": "3799\t\n3800\tenter @ 1.0s\n3801\t\n3802\tleft @ 1.6s\n3803\t\n3804\tmid-left @ 2.2s\n3805\t\n3806\tmid-right @ 2.8s\n3807\t\n3808\tright @ 3.4s\n3809\t\n3810\texit @ 4.0s\n3811\t\n3812\tvisual range proxy\n3813\t\n3814\tsound drift proxy\n3815\t\n3816\tstability = 94.9; SPL drift = 0.20 dB\n3817\t\n3818\tscore = 94.9\n3819\t\n3820\tFigure S5: Representative diagnostic readouts for Causality Violation, Range Attenuation, Approach Gain, and Lateral Stability.\n3821\tVisual-event or range evidence is shown at left, and the corresponding temporal or level-trajectory diagnostic is shown at right.\n3822\t\n3823\tS7.1\n3824\t\n3825\tOfficial T34 Subset and Pair Generation\n3826\t\n3827\tWe use five fixed near–far prompt pairs from PhyAVBench’s\n3828\tofficial T34 inverse-square-law subset (Xie et al. 2025). The\n3829\tsubset is selected from the official prompt file before generation rather than post-selected from model outputs. Within\n3830\teach pair, the scene, source, action, and fixed-camera description remain unchanged; only camera distance changes.\n3831\tSide A is the near condition and side B is the far condition.\n3832\tTable S14 lists the complete subset.\n3833\tFor every model and prompt pair, we use seeds 0–19 and\n3834\tapply the same seed to the near and far sides. The pairing\n3835\tkey is (model, sample ID, seed). This produces 5×20 = 100\n3836\tnear–far pairs and 200 generated videos per model. LTX-2.3,\n3837\tNAVA, and JavisDiT++ therefore contribute 100 pairs each,\n3838\t\n3839\tfor 300 pairs and 600 videos in total. Generation manifests\n3840\tverify that all 300 expected pairs contain both nonempty\n3841\tvideo files.\n3842\tAfter generation, AcoustiTrace does not assume that a\n3843\tmodel obeyed the camera distance written in the prompt.\n3844\tFor each A/B video, VDA-derived point-cloud export,\n3845\tGrounded-SAM source detection, and depth-track aggregation estimate the visible source distance. These estimates\n3846\tdefine\n3847\t∆Lexpected = −20 log10 (dB /dA ).\n3848\t(S16)\n3849\tWe extract mono audio at 16 kHz, compute global RMS\n3850\tlevels, and obtain ∆Lobserved = LB − LA . Their absolute\n3851\tdifference and the official near–far ordering provide the scalar\n3852\tAcoustiTrace score; event windows for discrete sources and\n3853\t\n3854\t\fEvaluation dimension\n3855\t\n3856\tAgreement [95% CI] (%)\n3857\t\n3858\tMotion–Loudness\n3859\tLog Attack Time\n3860\tImpact Decay\n3861\tRT60 Consistency\n3862\tCausality Violation\n3863\tRange Attenuation\n3864\tApproach Gain\n3865\tLateral Stability\n3866\t\n3867\t87.9 [81.41, 92.66]\n3868\t93.5 [88.01, 96.72]\n3869\t89.5 [83.21, 93.86]\n3870\t88.7 [82.31, 93.26]\n3871\t93.5 [88.01, 96.72]\n3872\t91.1 [85.16, 95.07]\n3873\t90.3 [84.26, 94.46]\n3874\t92.7 [87.11, 96.27]\n3875\t\n3876\tTable S13: Agreement between evaluator preferences and\n3877\thuman judgments on paired generated A/V samples, with\n3878\t95% confidence intervals.\n3879\t\n3880\tactive windows for continuous sources are retained as diagnostics. CPRS is computed independently on the same fixed\n3881\tpairs using only the official LAION-CLAP backend. The final table joins the two metrics by model, sample ID, seed,\n3882\tand A/B video paths, with no ImageBind scores included.\n3883\t\n3884\tS7.2\n3885\t\n3886\tModel-Wise Correlation\n3887\t\n3888\tBecause the relation-matched overlap comprises five fixed\n3889\tofficial prompt groups with 20 seeds per model, these correlations describe repeated generations on this fixed T34 subset\n3890\trather than a broader prompt population. The reported Fisher\n3891\tintervals are pair-level descriptive intervals conditional on\n3892\tthis fixed subset.\n3893\tAcross all 300 pairs, CPRS and AcoustiTrace exhibit only\n3894\tweak negative correlation. The model-wise estimates remain\n3895\tsmall and vary in sign or uncertainty: LTX-2.3 is near zero,\n3896\tNAVA has a weak negative Pearson correlation, and JavisDiT++ has a weak negative Spearman correlation. The result\n3897\tis therefore not driven by one model and does not support\n3898\ttreating either metric as a substitute for the other.\n3899\t\n3900\tS7.3\n3901\t\n3902\tRepresentative Disagreement Cases\n3903\t\n3904\tWe define high and low scores using 0.5 thresholds on\n3905\tboth axes and select three examples from each disagreement\n3906\tquadrant. Table S16 reports the final selected cases. Here\n3907\tErr∆L = |∆Lobserved − ∆Lexpected |, and Order equals one\n3908\twhen the generated far-side audio is quieter than the near-side\n3909\taudio under the official A/B assignment.\n3910\tAll three O cases violate the official near–far audio ordering and have attenuation errors from 1.81 to 4.29 dB\n3911\tdespite receiving CPRS above 0.5. Conversely, all three U\n3912\tcases preserve the ordering and match the visually inferred\n3913\tattenuation within 0.23 dB, yet receive CPRS below 0.5.\n3914\tFigure S6 shows sampled frames from the paired videos;\n3915\tthe metrics are computed from the complete generated A/V\n3916\tpairs rather than from these frames. These complementary errors explain the weak aggregate correlation: CPRS measures\n3917\tan embedding transition along its official reference direction, whereas AcoustiTrace tests the generated pair’s explicit\n3918\tvisual-distance and audio-attenuation relation.\n3919\t\n3920\tS8\n3921\t\n3922\tRange-Guided Audio Sampling Details\n3923\t\n3924\tThe benchmark’s main purpose is diagnosis, but a useful diagnostic should also expose a residual that can guide generation. We choose Range Attenuation for this proof of concept\n3925\tbecause its visual range trajectory and audio envelope define\n3926\ta continuous, differentiable relation, and a fixed recedingsource video supplies the same visual target to guided and\n3927\tunguided audio resampling. The experiment asks whether\n3928\tcorrecting that relation improves physical behavior without\n3929\treducing the test to the optimized score alone.\n3930\t\n3931\tS8.1\n3932\t\n3933\tGuidance Objective and Calibration\n3934\t\n3935\tThe intervention holds the canonical video fixed and resamples only the audio stream. Here, model refinement denotes\n3936\timproving the model’s inference-time generation process\n3937\tthrough diagnostic-guided sampling; no parameter update or\n3938\tretraining is involved. The same mechanism-level diagnoses\n3939\tcan also inform future training objectives, reward models,\n3940\tand candidate-selection strategies. The intervention does not\n3941\tmodify the prompt, regenerate the video, or change the Range\n3942\tAttenuation evaluator. Let di denote the relative visual range\n3943\tat time i and ei the differentiable envelope extracted from\n3944\tthe decoded mel-like representation. We optimize\n3945\t\u00152\n3946\tX\u0014\n3947\tei + ϵ\n3948\tdj + ϵ\n3949\tLdist =\n3950\tlog\n3951\t− γ log\n3952\t.\n3953\t(S17)\n3954\tej + ϵ\n3955\tdi + ϵ\n3956\ti,j\n3957\tHere γ specifies the target attenuation exponent; it is not the\n3958\tloss weight. We use γ = 1, corresponding to inverse-distance\n3959\tpressure or amplitude attenuation.\n3960\tDecoded-mel implementation At each denoising step, we\n3961\tunpatchify the audio x0 latent using an AudioPatchifier with\n3962\tpatch size one. The default latent has eight channels and 16\n3963\tmel bins. If zc,m,t denotes the Audio VAE decoder output,\n3964\tthe differentiable envelope is\n3965\txc,m,t = exp(clamp(zc,m,t , −20, 20)) ,\n3966\tq\n3967\t(S18)\n3968\t\u0003\n3969\t\u0002\n3970\tϵ = 10−6 .\n3971\tet = meanc,m x2c,m,t + ϵ,\n3972\tWe interpolate the canonical visual-distance trajectory to\n3973\tthis decoded mel time axis. All upper-triangular valid token pairs (i, j) are eligible; both endpoints must satisfy\n3974\tei , ej > 10−5 . When more than 4,096 pairs are available,\n3975\tdeterministic evenly spaced indices select 4,096 pairs rather\n3976\tthan randomly sampling pairs.\n3977\tEquation S17 presents the squared-residual form of the\n3978\tobjective. The implementation applies Smooth L1 loss with\n3979\tHuber transition β = 0.05 to the same log-ratio residual,\n3980\treducing the effect of isolated envelope tokens. We differentiate this decoded-mel loss with respect to audio x0 , update\n3981\tin the negative-gradient direction with guidance scale 5, and\n3982\tclip the update norm to 0.01∥x0 ∥.\n3983\tDecoded-mel proxy calibration We randomly sample 40\n3984\tclean, non-floor audio clips from a calibration pool disjoint\n3985\tfrom the 126 guidance-evaluation samples. The non-floor criterion excludes clips whose decoded envelopes reach the numerical floor required by the loss. For each retained clip, we\n3986\t\n3987\t\fRepresentative T34 CPRS / AcoustiTrace Disagreement Cases\n3988\tNear/Far frames are sampled from the generated videos; scores use final 300 CLAP-only pairs.\n3989\t\n3990\tO1 LTX2 m04_c13_t34_s02_g011 seed 6\n3991\t\n3992\tNear A\n3993\t\n3994\tFar B\n3995\t\n3996\tNear A\n3997\t\n3998\tFar B\n3999\t\n4000\tNear A\n4001\t\n4002\tFar B\n4003\t\n4004\tNear A\n4005\t\n4006\tFar B\n4007\t\n4008\tNear A\n4009\t\n4010\tFar B\n4011\t\n4012\tNear A\n4013\t\n4014\tFar B\n4015\t\n4016\tHigh CPRS / Low AcoustiTrace\n4017\tCPRS=0.707 AcoustiTrace=0.060 global error=4.29 dB\n4018\tnear/far order=0 expected=5.57 dB observed=1.28 dB\n4019\tFixed camera in a concrete stairwell. A person claps\n4020\tcontinuously, twice per second. Camera distance: 1 meter.\n4021\t\n4022\tO2 NAVA m04_c13_t34_s02_g011 seed 0\n4023\tHigh CPRS / Low AcoustiTrace\n4024\tCPRS=0.614 AcoustiTrace=0.000 global error=1.81 dB\n4025\tnear/far order=0 expected=-1.14 dB observed=0.68 dB\n4026\tFixed camera in a concrete stairwell. A person claps\n4027\tcontinuously, twice per second. Camera distance: 1 meter.\n4028\t\n4029\tO3 LTX2 m04_c13_t34_s02_g012 seed 11\n4030\tHigh CPRS / Low AcoustiTrace\n4031\tCPRS=0.561 AcoustiTrace=0.000 global error=3.13 dB\n4032\tnear/far order=0 expected=0.69 dB observed=3.82 dB\n4033\tIndoor fixed camera. A person taps the edge of a\n4034\tflat-bottomed metal pot with wooden chopsticks once per\n4035\tsecond. Camera distance: 0.5 meters.\n4036\t\n4037\tU1 LTX2 m04_c13_t34_s02_g020 seed 0\n4038\tLow CPRS / High AcoustiTrace\n4039\tCPRS=0.197 AcoustiTrace=0.992 global error=0.03 dB\n4040\tnear/far order=1 expected=-0.70 dB observed=-0.73 dB\n4041\tFixed camera on an outdoor road. A person is\n4042\thalf-squatting, holding a transparent plastic bottle\n4043\tfilled with water, and pours the water onto the ground\n4044\tfrom a height of about half a meter. Camera distance: 0.5\n4045\t\n4046\tU2 NAVA m04_c13_t34_s02_g012 seed 6\n4047\tLow CPRS / High AcoustiTrace\n4048\tCPRS=0.274 AcoustiTrace=0.936 global error=0.20 dB\n4049\tnear/far order=1 expected=-0.65 dB observed=-0.85 dB\n4050\tIndoor fixed camera. A person taps the edge of a\n4051\tflat-bottomed metal pot with wooden chopsticks once per\n4052\tsecond. Camera distance: 0.5 meters.\n4053\t\n4054\tU3 JavisDiT++ m04_c13_t34_s01_g021 seed 3\n4055\tLow CPRS / High AcoustiTrace\n4056\tCPRS=0.205 AcoustiTrace=0.771 global error=0.23 dB\n4057\tnear/far order=1 expected=0.08 dB observed=-0.16 dB\n4058\tFixed camera in a quiet room. A smartphone on a table\n4059\tplays a piano performance of \"Canon\" at a medium-low\n4060\tvolume. Camera distance: 1 meter.\n4061\t\n4062\tFigure S6: Representative CPRS–AcoustiTrace disagreement cases. Each row shows sampled near/far frames and the corresponding full-pair metrics. O1–O3 illustrate embedding over-credit; U1–U3 illustrate embedding under-credit relative to the\n4063\texplicit acoustic relation.\n4064\tsynthesize distances r ∈ {1, 1.5, 2, 3, 4}, scale the waveform\n4065\tby r0 /r, and pass every version through the same preprocessing, Audio VAE encoder, Audio VAE decoder, decoded-mel\n4066\tconversion, and envelope extraction used during 