e mean Spearman correlation shifts from −0.0880 to −0.4356, a paired change of\n4445\t−0.3476, indicating a stronger inverse relation between visual range and audio level.\n4446\tCLAP similarity increases by 0.0551 [0.0287, 0.0823].\n4447\tIn contrast, the integrated-LUFS interval includes zero, supporting that the intervention is not merely a global loudness adjustment. Audiobox PQ decreases from 6.106 without\n4448\tguidance to 5.884 with guidance, while remaining close to\n4449\tthe canonical value of 6.031. We therefore interpret the result\n4450\tas a targeted physical improvement with a modest predictedquality tradeoff rather than no degradation or an across-the-\n4451\t\n4452\tboard quality gain.\n4453\tThis single-model, single-relation intervention is an actionability proof of concept.\n4454\t\n4455\tReferences\n4456\tAspöck, L.; Brinkmann, F.; Ackermann, D.; Weinzierl, S.;\n4457\tand Vorländer, M. 2020. BRAS: Benchmark for Room\n4458\tAcoustical Simulation. Technische Universität Berlin, DepositOnce. Version 3.\n4459\tBrinkmann, F.; Aspöck, L.; Ackermann, D.; Opdam, R.;\n4460\tVorländer, M.; and Weinzierl, S. 2021. A Benchmark for\n4461\tRoom Acoustical Simulation: Concept and Database. Applied Acoustics, 176: 107867.\n4462\t\n4463\t\fChang, A. X.; Dai, A.; Funkhouser, T.; Halber, M.; Niessner, M.; Savva, M.; Song, S.; Zeng, A.; and Zhang, Y. 2017.\n4464\tMatterport3D: Learning from RGB-D Data in Indoor Environments. In 2017 International Conference on 3D Vision,\n4465\t667–676.\n4466\tChen, C.; Schissler, C.; Garg, S.; Kobernik, P.; Clegg, A.;\n4467\tCalamia, P.; Batra, D.; Robinson, P.; and Grauman, K. 2022.\n4468\tSoundSpaces 2.0: A Simulation Platform for Visual-Acoustic\n4469\tLearning. In Advances in Neural Information Processing\n4470\tSystems, volume 35, 8896–8911.\n4471\tCui, Z.; Liu, X.; Fang, H.; Xu, M.; Liu, J.; Xu, Z.; Pian,\n4472\tW.; Deng, S.; Du, F.; Ge, C.; and Tian, Y. 2026. Do\n4473\tJoint Audio-Video Generation Models Understand Physics